window.harnessChunks["rows-dc13bbe704c20628.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:09:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">INIT-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent/tree/1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\">1b8b407 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Convert coding requests into tool-mediated work, revise actions after observed outcomes and return results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code/task state, model results, tool failures, user steering and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select subsequent code/tool actions and responses from the model&#39;s observations. - Decision owner: Mutable agent-body model in the normal supported session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model I/O types, tool dispatcher, context/log, kernel process lifecycle, Root sandbox and egress proxy. - Closure path: Input/context → model-selected tool call → dispatched tool result in the conversation → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: The installed init-agent launches the shipped body loop through the kernel; tool use is not borrowed from the provider&#39;s internal agent service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party model chooses substantive tool use and adjusts after feedback; Root only constrains execution and supplies transport. - Evidence: seed loop, model tool-call types, tool dispatcher, body boot. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection only; no paid provider or installed-session E2E execution was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop at this chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential interference among multiple independent S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific inter-S1 conflict-resolution right established. - Decision owner: None evidenced for S2 in the normal distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded delegation, serial body turn execution, process/sandbox separation and ordinary result return. - Closure path: Delegation and result reporting support S1 task decomposition, without an evidenced S2-specific coordination correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple processes and a delegate tool do not independently demonstrate an interference/oscillation witness and subsequent coordination feedback. - Evidence: delegate implementation, delegate tool, system architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This does not assert that a custom future fleet cannot implement S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent-controlled current recovery and runtime constraint changes over the one live agent-and-Root system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe operation, health regressions, continued erroneous work, spend/exposure concerns and failed self-changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect system status and select owner panic/stop/recovery/rollback rather than simply accept the current run. - Decision owner: Legitimate human operator via the Root&#39;s owner channel; Root executes the returned choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root status snapshot, owner socket/CLI, signed lineage, sandbox, watchdog and snapshot restore. - Closure path: Root current status and warnings → owner chooses panic, stop, resume or rollback → Root changes currently permitted execution/recovered tuple. - Boundary reachability: init-root status, attach, panic, resume and Root recovery are supported installed owner commands, not contributor CI or a suggested external management plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent selects consequential whole-system intervention; deterministic Root enforcement and autonomous threshold rollback alone are not credited as agent-owned S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: Root publishes live slot/mode/level/charter, process state, spend, snapshots and fault/lineage status for the deployed system. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume current work and restore/restrict the active body state and access envelope. - Evidence: Root owner CLI, Root contract, README controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Parent path is local owner control, not an autonomous multi-agent S3 or organizational governance of repository contributors.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: A Root-sealed, agent-inaccessible regression check of evolved bodies is present; its ownership as the Methodology&#39;s discretionary complementary S3 audit remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported successful mutation concealing functional regression or benchmark gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Challenge the current body with unseen tasks and return regression/rollback decisions. - Decision owner: Sealed deterministic Root evaluator executes tests and thresholds; no separately evidenced autonomous discretionary audit judge is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root-only sealed task store, canaries, paired comparisons, noise floors, signed audit records and rollback mechanism. - Closure path: Sealed task results → deterministic regression verdict → rollback/demotion or accepted audited pointer, affecting subsequent body operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Complementary first-party access and correction exist, but the recorded judge is deterministic enforcement. Further bounded analysis is needed to decide whether the established function&#39;s published ownership is C, — or unresolved under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: Root sealed evaluation contract, Root module wiring, promotion gate contract. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: low for ownership, high for shipped sealed infrastructure. - Caveats: This is not an independent reviewer run or proof of successful model performance.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Propose and deploy changes to the agent&#39;s future operating capabilities from observed task/tool limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repeated tool failures, inefficiencies and capability mismatch with prospective work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses edits to its own prompt, extension/tool set or compiled body and requests their promotion. - Decision owner: Agent model authors the adaptation option; Root/kernel safety and performance gates may reject it without owning option generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable body/extension worktree, initctl promote, G0–G4 tests, staged process hot-swap, lineage and safety rollback. - Closure path: Observed limitations → agent-authored source/extension change → promotion/gates → accepted live body/tool capability used on later turns; rejected change returns evidence for another attempt. - Boundary reachability: Self-edit is a documented native agent capability; released kernel/Root perform the promotion and can preserve conversation state through a core swap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source modification and future option are model-selected; a fixed compiler/test/holdout guard has veto power but does not independently choose the proposed enhancement. - External distinction: Performance and tool demands of tasks/environment relative to present agent capabilities. - Future / prospective distinction: Revised future tool/core behavior, not merely saving a current conversation. - Adaptation option generated: New/modified skills, tools and Rust body code. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted promotion changes the running body; Root can roll it back based on current outcome. - Evidence: README self-evolution workflow, agent evolution contract, kernel gate contract, extension authoring. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Shipped self-edit closure is not a claim of measured net capability gain; the README explicitly notes that persistent benefit is not yet demonstrated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Keep ultimate authority over the agent&#39;s identity and non-editable safety/extension charter outside the self-modifying actor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent efforts to redefine its own authority, protected access and evolution permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and explicitly apply the Root charter defining the owner&#39;s non-negotiable authority envelope. - Decision owner: Human owner; the independent Root validates, signs and enforces the choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-only charter.toml, charter checks/digest, active charter state, Root sandbox/permission rules and separation from the agent zone. - Closure path: Owner changes charter and invokes charter check --apply → Root validates/activates it → the next runtime decisions are constrained by the new authoritative charter. - Boundary reachability: The installed Root CLI exposes owner-only charter check/apply while the mutable agent is sandboxed away from Root home. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The self-modifying agent does not control the protected charter or Root. Runtime permission requests alone were not counted; the evidenced claim is the explicit higher-level charter authority and return to operation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether and under which hard non-editable constraints the agent may alter itself, gain access or survive a regression. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human owner only; Root records/enforces the decision. - Return-to-operation path: Active charter affects subsequent agent-zone sandbox and promotion operations. - Evidence: Root charter implementation, owner apply CLI, Root architecture. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Does not infer project-level S5 governance from the existence of maintainers, contributors or release approvals.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1/assessments/in/init-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1\">061d70e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:56:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sora Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness/tree/cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\">cbfddaf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce local tool-mediated task results, translations and project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: input formats, provider/tool errors, partial results, project state and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose operations/tools and revise task work after observed results. - Decision owner: the model-backed main actor and supported child actors in their respective task scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentLoop, provider backends, tool registry, workspace restrictions, step/call limits, checkpoints and subprocess execution. - Closure path: tool results append to model history; errors or partial state lead to revised actions, while terminal results are persisted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actors leaves wrappers/storage/guards without discretionary problem solving. - Evidence: desktop/runtime/agent/loop.py; desktop/application/conversations.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/tools/registry.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: ConversationApplication invokes the first-party AgentLoop; the child runner constructs that same application and sends/resumes a message.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent logical and physical write interference between concurrent parent/child operational tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers sharing a project can overwrite the same target or publish partial/inconsistent translation packages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint work/target-ID ranges and separate result paths, revise overlapping scopes and decide serialized final publication. - Decision owner: the main model actor following the supplied subagent/tool and collaboration mode; locks only enforce exclusive writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped subagent tools, actual child status/results, send/continue messages, shared mutation-lock propagation and file-lock serialization. - Closure path: the main actor reads actual results, checks missing/duplicate/out-of-range IDs and file contents, corrects directly or sends revised child instructions, then commits the reconciled package serially. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the main actor a lock can serialize two incompatible edits but cannot choose semantically disjoint task boundaries or reconcile their outputs. - Evidence: desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/application/mutationgate.py; desktop/tools/registry.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py; desktop/application/skills.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Optional enabled multi-actor mode, not every single-agent chat. Configured child models/profiles are normal deployment inputs. The lock alone would not justify A. - Distinct S1 units: main task actor and independent-history child translation, review or research task actors with separate outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace writes, conflicting edits to the same target and overlapping or incomplete target-ID coverage. - Attenuating coordination relation: agent-selected non-overlapping ranges/result files, central serial apply/build and corrective scope messages, backed by cross-process write exclusion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: real child states and artifact coverage checks cause corrected delegation/continuation or revised publication decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party tool instructions and collaboration rules explicitly identify shared-file/logical collisions and complete-ID publication, rather than merely exposing a generic mailbox. - Boundary reachability: enabled subagent profiles bind real tool capabilities in SubagentManager; the built-in tool description supplies coordination rules to the model. The translation plugin&#39;s skill root and SkillService make the concrete disjoint-ID/serial-commit workflow available in the supported product mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No supplied whole-application discretionary current-control function is established beyond bounded task coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child runtime limits, failed work and local task progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent status/cancel/continue, per-role step/time/call limits, run records and main-agent task aggregation. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/runs.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent complementary audit path is supplied beyond ordinary operational review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete child claims and translation/schema errors are checked in the production workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actual-file reads, acceptance reports, review subtasks, tool receipts and run/checkpoint logs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md; skills/game-translation/references/candidate-review.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/session.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Independent histories and raw-file availability alone do not turn every review into S3. This is bounded to the supplied product/skill modes, not a prohibition on a custom audit design.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported game layouts, context limits and task-relevant reference information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin/skill loading, retrieval, compaction and project-local adapter authoring. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/custom-adapters.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/application/skills.py; desktop/runtime/context/compaction.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary user task scope, prompt presets, permissions and plugin enablement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt configuration, user questions/steering, per-profile instructions and tool allowlists. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/prompts/config.py; desktop/prompts/assembly.py; desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/tools/contracts.py; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68/assessments/so/sora-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68\">de3f319 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T19:04:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Enterprise Agent Harness Template</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template/tree/02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\">02d40b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct research, coding and review specialists perform model-selected work for their assigned subtasks; supervisor itself uses a model-directed delegation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Research/document changes, coding outcomes, partial/incorrect drafts, tool responses, task-specific failures and approval delays. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each specialist model chooses its action or answer from a fresh subtask prompt and any available permitted tools; the supervisor model chooses delegate tool calls and final composition. - Decision owner: Configured specialist agent model at each child-run S1 boundary, implemented via the runnable AgentLoop/tool pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent YAML model/tool/limits, isolated child contexts, standard tool execution/guards, checkpoints, retries/recovery and explicit RunState outcomes. - Closure path: Parent task → model-chosen delegate call → distinct child model/tool/observation loop → child result/error → parent feedback/next decision. - Boundary reachability: First-party Harness.build installs delegation and validates shipped supervisor YAML&#39;s specialist names; delegate calls createrun/execute, not merely an example external orchestrator. Integration tests exercise parallel independent children with fake LLMs; configured production inference endpoints were not exercised here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Child models own discretionary task steps; the host executes checks and records state. The supervisor&#39;s choice of subtask does not erase independent specialist S1 agency. - Evidence: loop, runtime wiring, child registry, integration fixture. - Basis: Installed separate autonomous child-run model/tool feedback, with source and test-case corroboration. - Confidence: High for source-distribution reachability; runtime paid-model outcome not independently verified. - Caveats: Child availability and model quality depend on operator configuration; test assertions with FakeLLM prove paths rather than live autonomy quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: A specific installed attenuating channel for inter-S1 operational conflict is not demonstrated, despite real sub-agent delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping specialist tasks, incompatible answers or resource contention could arise, but the source does not encode detection/settlement of reciprocal S1 clashes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisor can assign and repeat work, and static registry validates allowed children; no evidence of separate horizontal S1-to-S1 disturbance arbitration or an S2-specific feedback relation. - Decision owner: Supervisor model as current task manager; deterministic roster/permission validation handles routing, not inter-unit conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagents allowlist, parallel delegate calls, child IDs and shared tenant/tools with separate contexts. - Closure path: Parent delegation → child replies → supervisor current control; no distinct conflict signal between S1 children → attenuating settlement → reciprocal changed operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegation, tool routing, serial/parallel sequencing and shared budget accounting alone do not establish the VSM S2 function. - Evidence: delegation, supervisor, integration tests. - Basis: Bounded absence review against the strongest multi-agent candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high; positive S3 supervision is not automatically positive S2 conflict attenuation. - Caveats: A future operator policy could require or build explicit inter-specialist conflict arbitration, but no independent pathway is currently closed in the shipped model.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: A live supervisor controls the present specialist-work portfolio for one parent task by selecting subagents, reviewing outcomes and adjusting assignments under a shared task budget. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse subtasks, inadequate specialist output, failed/paused child runs, concurrent execution and aggregate token/cost limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven supervisor chooses which allowed specialist gets each subtask (possibly in parallel), sees child answers/errors/pause status, can delegate corrective work, decides when to synthesize the overall answer. - Decision owner: Configured supervisor model; installed delegate tool and harness enforce its chosen child-run requests and report results. The static budget cap is not itself the S3 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervisor.yaml and supervisor@v1 instructions, child roster, depth ceiling, deterministic child IDs for crash recovery, rolled-up child usage/cost, isolated subtask execution and returned status/answer observations. - Closure path: Supervisor task view → delegate choices → child S1 task execution → successful/error/awaiting-approval tool outcome → supervisor&#39;s next model turn and re-delegation/answer choice, with aggregate cost/usage enforced by parent loop. - Whole-system current view: At the declared parent-task recursion, the supervisor sees its approved specialist roster and actual result/error messages from each dispatched child; shared parent budget counts child usage. It does not see all users/tenants or manage an enterprise fleet. - Current-control decision scope: Current assignment, follow-up/reassignment, review sequencing and acceptance/synthesis of the research/coding/review portfolio, not long-term system strategy or ultimate-policy rights. - Boundary reachability: The first-party composition root installs the delegate registry, shipped supervisor config enumerates specialists, prompt explicitly directs re-delegation on bad results, and integration test runs independent children concurrently and returns results/cost to the parent. This is operational first-party S3 for the bounded task organization, not the mere class name Supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model can choose or revise present portfolio assignments after actual child feedback. Deterministic guard/leases/idempotency alone would not qualify; positive mapping depends on the installed decision path plus child autonomy. - Evidence: supervisor instructions, shipped roster, delegate return, tests. - Basis: Evidence-backed installed supervisory current-control closed loop across autonomous child work, sharply scoped to one task organization. - Confidence: Medium-high (decision-path closure proven at source; live provider correctness and conflict handling not tested). - Caveats: A supervisor may simply accept poor outputs; normative quality of its choices is untested and S3 is not S3 independent audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary reality-checking auditor verifies task claims via alternate evidence and closes findings into current management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad research claims or coding changes can be questioned by review specialist or offline eval regression tests, but their installed evidence paths do not make an independent live audit circuit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: review agent has no first-party truth-access tools and receives only a supervisor-authored subtask prompt; audit log hashes observed events without verifying their truth. Eval runner is a separate test/CI workflow, not a returned S3 corrective channel for live work. - Decision owner: Supervisor decides whether to request review and whether to act on it; review model lacks independent access to workspace/reality evidence, offline scorer/CI operator lives outside the task controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional review sub-agent, tamper-evident AuditLog.verify, policy safety guard, offline test expectations and regression baseline comparison. - Closure path: Supervisor-provided material → review critique → supervisor continuation (an ordinary S1 report); or offline test → regression report. Neither creates independent complementary evidence → audit verdict → enforceable S3 correction through an accountable audit role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate model identity alone does not imply independent observational access; hash-chain integrity only proves log continuity, and a CI pass is not an independent audit of actual deployment claims. - Evidence: review spec, audit log, evals, delegation. - Basis: Explicit separation of reviewer model, audit-log transport and offline eval against required S3 complementary-independence conditions. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An operator could independently audit via external tools/records; the current review agent may use its learned knowledge but has no distinct first-party reality observation path in its YAML.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct future-environment scanning/adaptation authority governs the whole task organization beyond current user work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider availability, changing task types, tool/schema policy, evolving skill capability and future usage forecasts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator controls agent YAML/provider/plugin updates; specialist research serves current delegated question rather than strategic capability change. - Decision owner: Developer/tenant for future architecture; supervisor for present delegation only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin extension points, config reload/deployment, memory stores, eval baselines and model/provider retries. - Closure path: Tasks and current tool feedback → user answer or manual version update; no autonomous prospective environmental intelligence → option selection → S3 negotiation/adopted future capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A research sub-agent and memory embeddings may help present tasks, but do not install an organizational future-modeling/strategy actor. - Evidence: architecture, research spec, runtime. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Tool-using agents can discuss the future within individual tasks; that is not a shipped S4 organizational feedback loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/policy deliberation or legitimate parent-governed identity-level mandate returns to the supervisor-led organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Security permissions, sensitive data, tool use, tenant accountability and human approval requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy YAML and operator settings define guards; human reviewer approves/denies individual risky tool calls and may be prohibited from approving their own call. None evidences identity-level policy revision. - Decision owner: External operator/security policy author for static mandates and approval reviewer for individual actions, not a packaged S5 ultimate governance role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SafetyGuard, default policy, ApprovalService, four-eyes switch, audit log, data redaction and tenant API authentication. - Closure path: Policy rule → tool pause → human yes/no → resumed or denied action. This is a closed ordinary permission loop, not identity/mission issue → ultimate authority → revised governance mandate to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rigorous approvals and tamper-evident security logging enforce chosen policy, but do not confer first-party S5 or the Methodology&#39;s parent-mode P without a function-specific identity decision right. - Evidence: approvals, guard, policy. - Basis: Bounded absence review distinguishing action authorization from organizational identity-policy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: In a real enterprise, external human governance likely exists; it is not an encoded operational S5 mode within this shipped template.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96/assessments/en/enterprise-agent-harness-template.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96\">c962f1e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:16:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dougong</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong/tree/5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\">5d7ff99 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed problem solving through iterative use of file, search, shell, MCP and delegated task tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown workspace state, model responses, tool errors, user task changes and subprocess results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools and arguments, delegate self-contained subtasks, interpret results and select the next task action or final response. - Decision owner: Primary and child model-backed agent loops; the host supplies protocols but not the concrete solution steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kernel registration, tool dispatcher, approval policy, JSONL session commit, schema validation, workspace path guard, model connectors, retry and tool-round limits. - Closure path: Prompt/history → IChatClient completion → model-selected tool call → dispatcher execution → tool/child result → subsequent IChatClient turn and final task output. - Boundary reachability: Default SessionComposition registers tools and creates LoopAgentRuntime in both CLI and ACP paths; SubagentTool uses the same runtime class with fresh child history. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model choice leaves permission enforcement and protocol handling, but no substantive next-step/task-selection discretion. - Evidence: LoopAgentRuntime.RunAsync, SessionComposition tool registration, ToolDispatcher. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired model/tool feedback; external provider outcome not executed during this review. - Caveats: Offline probes and advertised provider benchmarks are not used as evidence of real-task completion rates.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate contention between concurrent task-owning sessions or worker calls accessing the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping shell/tool mutation and inconsistent shared-workspace state when independently executing S1 cells interfere; specifically two supported ACP sessions entering exclusive workspace actions. - Distinct S1 units: Two concurrently model-operated ACP sessions in one process, and primary/child independent task loops when their scoped tools overlap. - Inter-S1 disturbance: First-party G9 case explicitly models two session gates entering the same workspace and risking simultaneous exclusive operations; unguarded writes can overlap. - Attenuating coordination relation: Process-shared normalized workspace mutex for serial/exclusive tools; a per-dispatcher resource-key read/write gate for declared tool resources; execution lease delays conflicting calls. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The blocked call executes only after the lease clears, then its concrete tool result reaches its requesting agent&#39;s next model turn. The coordination changes the timing/observable world state of subsequent operations; refusal/error is also returned through dispatcher. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The gate explicitly targets a concrete conflict among autonomous work cells (same workspace competing tool mutations), with conflict-dependent exclusion and resumed tool feedback; it is not merely a queue of tasks or parent-to-child dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Apply or revise the actual inter-session resource-exclusion relation. The supplied resource gate deterministically applies declared plans; no autonomous agent is shipped as an owner of shared conflict-policy discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic constructor/runtime resource gate; developers declare tool concurrency and can compose policy authority. Agent models choose tasks but do not decide lock admissibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SharedWorkspaceLocks, semaphores, stable resource keys, registry limits and ToolExecutionPlan. - Closure path: Competing tool intents → gate classification and lease acquisition → serialized protected execution → result back to the relevant agent; construction of an adaptive autonomous coordination owner is not part of this standard distribution. - Boundary reachability: CLI/ACP SessionComposition constructs a non-isolated ToolResourceGate; the static shared lock table is reached by serial/workspace-exclusive tool invocations, corroborated by pinned G9 probe. Resource-key locks are instance-scoped and are not claimed to protect arbitrary file edits across independent dispatchers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing agents&#39; discretionary policy leaves the same lock decision and resource exclusion; the function-specific path is real, but not an agent-owned S2 closure, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: ToolResourceGate, G9 cross-session counterproof, SubagentTool resource classification, host wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded coordination; not a claim of universal cross-worker file conflict protection. - Caveats: Asynchronously spawned children outlive the spawn-tool lease; cross-process locks and all resource-key paths are not established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Allocate and revise current task commitments across the primary agent&#39;s active child-agent work portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel worker availability, failed delegated results, conflicting subtask priorities and finite execution capacity during one task composition. - Whole-system current view: Agent-accessible subagentlist exposes all handles in this composition with current running state, and subagentresult returns terminal status, text, error and consumed tool rounds; getgoal and joblist expose current local task/goal state. - Current-control decision scope: Primary model chooses which child work to launch or postpone, which results to retrieve, whether to execute remaining work itself and what follow-up action to assign, within a four-concurrent-child and bounded-depth envelope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commit or revise currently active work distribution based on live child and goal results. - Decision owner: Primary conversational model; SubagentRegistry and goal/dispatcher implement its choices but do not choose which task to prioritize. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child handles, concurrency gate, depth bounds, one-shot result collection, goal state, optional job status and tool permission checks. - Closure path: Primary chooses subtask → asynchronous child run → model requests list/result → receives current status/diagnostic → decides to delegate another task, wait, adjust, work directly or finish in a subsequent loop iteration. - Boundary reachability: SessionComposition registers subagent, subagentlist, subagentresult and goal tools with the standard CLI/ACP model dispatcher; the supplied child loop uses the same IChatClient and shared registry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove primary model discretion and the runtime still enforces slots and reports status but does not decide task allocation, current commitments or recovery. - Evidence: SubagentTools, SubagentRegistry, SessionComposition registration and child runtime. - Basis: structural; agent discretion in the standard tool-call loop is explicit. - Confidence: medium for current-control scope, high for callable feedback path. - Caveats: S3 scope is the one primary-session task portfolio, not a global dashboard controlling all separately instantiated hosts. No parent-governed S3 mode is evidenced by the optional plan approver.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary direct-reality audit that reports corrective findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failure, approval denial and stale session reports are ordinarily visible, but no separate auditor authority samples them independently. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge the operational claim with materially different access and return corrective findings to S3; not wired. - Decision owner: Ordinary dispatcher and logger record their own actions; a human plan approver can accept a proposed plan, not audit completed operational claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSONL commit barrier, ToolDispatcher approval logs, user plan review, diagnostics, self-check comparison suite and subagent result summaries. - Closure path: Tool status returns directly to requesting model or is stored for replay; no independent auditor sampling → judgment → corrective returned control loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A child can hypothetically be tasked to review a patch, but the default host has no protected independent reviewer role, separate evidence path or mandatory corrective audit closure. - Evidence: JsonlAgentStore, ToolDispatcher, PlanMode, SubagentTools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: External code reviewers and offline self-check probes are outside the assessed operational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped, operationally closed external-prospective capability adaptation process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/provider changes, evolving user preferences and plugin capability opportunities could merit adaptation, but the default loop addresses current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluate future-relevant environmental options and authorize capability change returned to current S3/operations; not supplied as a complete first-party chain. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies tool plugins, provider and preferences; models choose available current task actions and can write plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin lifecycle/hot swap, skill discovery, optional L1 MemoryStore/MemoryInjector, session compaction and manual config. - Closure path: Whitelisted Enabled memory would be injected into later turns, but the shipped CLI has no writer invoking MemoryStore.PutAsync/AdvanceCursorAsync to create, judge, approve and enable new L1 artifacts from outside/future evidence. No prospective option judgment and capability change loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Temporal persistence and dynamic plugin loading do not make a model-owned strategic adaptation decision; optional memory readback is a support surface only. - Evidence: MemoryArtifact, MemoryInjector, SessionComposition, README.en L1 claims. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: The separate experiment proving preference adherence does not establish a deployed preference-learning decision owner; embedded SDK consumers could add the missing cycle independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime resolution of organizational identity or ultimate-policy dilemmas with authoritative return into subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous tools, security/privacy switches, permission escalation and plan approval are execution risks within a preselected organizational purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a contested identity-level/ultimate-policy matter and enact the new constitutional constraint in the operating harness; no such first-party path. - Decision owner: User/operator configures permission/environment, human approver authorizes individual tool or plan transitions, and developer chooses fixed policy implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SecuritySwitches, PermissionPolicyApprover, approval content binding, restricted L1 preference whitelist and plan review. - Closure path: User-approved actions change current task execution; configuration fixed at startup bounds tool access. No identity/policy proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → governing response → durable changed operation loop exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Approval is local task authorization; neither model nor operator tool prompt resolves identity or ultimate policy of the operating organization. - Evidence: PermissionPolicyApprover, PlanMode, SecuritySwitches via host, MemoryArtifact whitelist. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: Maintainer repository-level governance is outside the deployed runtime organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138/assessments/do/dougong.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138\">1ab3c93 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:53:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DynaFlow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow/tree/7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\">7451639 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-driven subagent interprets a delegated question subtask, chooses its own supported research/tool calls and produces a substantive answer or structured result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain source material, changing factual evidence, calculations, research/tool response differences and errors in executing an assigned subproblem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to query configured tools, reason about their actual outputs, retry task-local reasoning and deliver a substantive result to the workflow. - Decision owner: the DynaFlow-managed subagent model invoked through Session.worker and the agent() hook; the independent model API service supplies computation but its unrelated internal policies are not borrowed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflowworker.system.txt role constraints, model-compatible Chat Completions adapter, allowed tool schemas, Python executetool bridge, Docker credential isolation, output schema validation, journal and progress events. - Closure path: main workflow delegates subtask → configured worker model interprets prompt and requests relevant tools → tool/model results return into its message history → final text/StructuredOutput is delivered or explicitly marked dead. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/workflow.py provides a real worker model-call loop and Python host tool execution, while workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts runs the worker and returns validated results to the JavaScript script in supported dynamic/forced modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host cannot perform task-specific research/calculate the final substantive result by parsing the static workflow alone; the model-driven worker makes nontrivial task decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/systemprompts.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts, README mode table. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reachable owned subagent execution, not for actual answer accuracy on unrun providers. - Caveats: model serving and externally configured search APIs are separate providers; a default direct baseline is not the basis for counting multi-agent role ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct lateral inter-S1 disturbance attenuation and return path beyond workflow scheduling/resource constraints is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent requests contend for a configured maximum number of execution slots and tokens, but no independent cross-worker semantic collision or negotiated working relationship is specified in the shipped path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the semaphore grants/reclaims permits, the engine numbers calls, and script dependencies order tasks; these are constructor-authored scheduling controls, not a supported S2 conflict-resolution right over peer S1 relations. - Decision owner: static engine limits and caller-authored workflow script, not an identifiable lateral coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Semaphore.acquire/release, global agent cap, shared budget, Promise.all-style workflow orchestration, journal sequence and cancellation. - Closure path: one call awaits capacity and later proceeds when a permit is released; this closes a bounded scheduler resource constraint without showing disturbance-specific feedback changing separate S1 units&#39; conflicting operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and a concurrency cap do not automatically establish the S2 function; no source-evidenced first-party peer contention arbitration/reconciliation was identified. - Distinct S1 units: the individually prompted subagents called by the generated workflow. - Inter-S1 disturbance: potential competition for capacity or contradictory research outputs, but a distinct implemented peer conflict path is not shown. - Attenuating coordination relation: configured semaphore and script dependency sequencing; neither is evidence of an S2-specific cross-worker conflict feedback loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a waiting slot delays launch, but no path-specific conflict ruling is carried to an already operating peer to regulate its subsequent activity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: no S2-specific evidence survives this distinction; hence absence rather than an inflated S2=C score. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/concurrency.ts, hooks.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high at the frozen shipped boundary. - Caveats: a user-generated Workflow could voluntarily implement specialized peer coordination, but arbitrary user-authored scripts are not a guaranteed first-party function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: the main model dynamically plans, launches, monitors, assesses and modifies the current subagent Workflow to complete the operator&#39;s question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed subagents, incomplete/invalid delivery, insufficient evidence, changing research direction, stalled/aborted work and uncertain selection between direct and delegated execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate task-specific workflow scripts; choose serial/parallel subagent composition and verification stages; call Workflow/TaskOutput/TaskStop; revise or rerun workflow when receipts indicate incomplete or contradicted evidence; decide whether to synthesize the final answer. - Decision owner: first-party-instructed main model in Session.run, in the supported dynamic/forced runtime; deterministic engine/host rules act as subordinate gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: main role prompt, mode/forced policy, tool schemas and asynchronous Session.launch/output, status receipts, journal resume, bounded retries, deadline/cancel paths and host&#39;s valid-cloud-agent finalization predicate. - Closure path: user question and current execution constraints → main model chooses/generates a Workflow → subagents work and send progress/results → TaskOutput/delivery/status returned → main model revises/reruns/stops or completes answer under immutable host acceptance rules. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt and forcedprompt.txt require the described lead action; src/dynaflow/workflow.py exposes the actual tool calls, stores and returns receipts, and supports re-launch/resume and output blocking. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike the fixed semaphore or outer question queue, the model chooses and changes the task-specific subagent program and how to respond to unsatisfactory execution, giving it a bona fide current whole-workflow management right. - Whole-system current view: active local Workflow invocations, completed run IDs/receipts, failed or valid delivery, current subagent responses and original question acceptance requirements. - Current-control decision scope: question-local orchestration/recovery/answer readiness; not global resource governance over multiple independently funded projects or stable policy change. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py (Session.run, launch, output), first-party main/forced prompts, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the supported main-agent-in-the-loop operating mode. - Caveats: actual use of the optional dynamic Workflow is conditional; in direct, no subagent organization exists. The model decides current workflow design, but cannot bypass host tool restriction, budget, delivery validity or user stop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately tasked, independently evidenced challenge of an operating agent&#39;s correctness claim with a mandated corrective feedback return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: malformed JSON, empty/invalid deliveries and model outputs lacking required successful-agent provenance are caught, but these are validity/reachability guards, not independent evidence-based challenge of substantive claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: constructor code checks schemas, transport receipts and whether a successful configured subagent delivered results; no distinct substantive reviewer actor is given a protected right to inspect contradicting primary evidence and return adjudicated findings. - Decision owner: deterministic validateAgentDelivery and host finalization predicates for format/provenance; model-driven main agent may inspect results but is also author of the plan and final synthesis, not an independent assessor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: StructuredOutput JSON Schema, validateDelivery, validcloudagents, TaskOutput, run journal and optional user-created script verification steps. - Closure path: subagent output → schema/empty-result and successful-delivery checks → accepted/rejected receipt or current orchestration re-run; no default complementary channel challenges whether the answer is true against independently obtained source/acceptance evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: syntactic validation is constructor logic, and ad hoc optional model-generated verification does not establish a separate first-party audit mandate or epistemic independence. - Claim being audited: supposed successful and correct response to the original research question. - Ordinary reporting path: subagent task output/status delivered through the host. - Complementary access path: no standard required reviewer with its own independent primary-source acquisition; the main model can read task outputs and optionally design a verification stage. - Independence boundary: no deployed distinct reviewer identity, read-only isolation or mandatory independently sourced cross-check shown. - Who acts on findings: the main agent may rerun after failed delivery, but that is ordinary current-control recovery without an independently warranted challenge verdict. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/deliveryValidation.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific generated script could use separate verification subagents and alter a future task-specific score, but existence of arbitrary script capability is not evidence of a shipped S3 audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no ongoing first-party environment intelligence function that anticipates changes and modifies the longer-term capabilities/strategy of the DynaFlow work organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel user questions and alternate model research sources are present-work inputs, not demonstrated changes in future capability demand. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses an appropriate Workflow for a single question, and developers may change runtime code, but no bounded S4 agent authorizes prospective operating capability adaptations. - Decision owner: question-local main model for current-work plan and external maintainers/users for updates or future workloads. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamically generated workflow scripts, run records, mode configuration and academic research documentation. - Closure path: incoming question → generated current-work script → result; no current environmental trend → prospective adaptation proposal → installed new future organizational capability loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dynamic per-task program generation is not automatically S4; the generated script executes and is discarded/archived as that task&#39;s procedure, rather than constituting a persistent policy/capability shift. - Evidence: pinned README, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human research team may analyze DynaFlow experiments to change later algorithms, outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internally closed legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision function for a DynaFlow installation is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API credentials, allowed research tools, direct-versus-workflow policy, Docker capability restrictions, quotas and required delivery are fixed by constructor configuration/user mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: mode selection and engine constraints come from administrator/configuration; main model cannot rewrite the execution rules or determine ultimate purpose outside the supplied question. - Decision owner: external deployer/evaluation owner and static constructor policy rather than an autonomous organization-level constitutional actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompt, configured tool list, credential isolation, Docker network/privilege restrictions, budget/agent caps and STOPBYUSER marker. - Closure path: externally specified policy → enforced model/tool permissions and delivery constraints; no legitimate identity/ultimate-policy dispute is adjudicated and returned to govern the future organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: obeying a forced mode and enforcing security restrictions are real constraints, but not first-party S5 political/identity decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/configuration.py, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, src/dynaflow/assets/forcedprompt.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high for the narrow supplied runtime. - Caveats: the paper authors and external academic laboratory have policy and strategy outside this executable product; these are not imported as its first-party S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297/assessments/dy/dynaflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297\">83a9181 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:33:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ruslantrachuk/dark-factory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dark factory</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ruslantrachuk/dark-factory/tree/a56142f7e79efdfa9aa8acee7596974b4efea174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a56142f7e79efdfa9aa8acee7596974b4efea174\">a56142f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver project changes and bounded correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: underspecified implementation details, code/test failures and returned findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation, tests and bounded fixes within the approved request. - Decision owner: first-party builder role instantiated by the installed delivery workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external host/model, source-defined role and skills, scoped worktree/tools, schemas, tests, commit and task-state scripts. - Closure path: owned builder prompt → edits/tests/result → integration and review → fix agent or explicit incomplete result → committed branch. - Boundary reachability: feature command and feature-pipeline manifest invoke shipped named workflows and builder role; no private author runtime is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow scaffolding alone cannot implement arbitrary requirements; the configured builder exercises local coding discretion. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-auto.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/agents/builder.md; plugins/dark-factory/templates/feature-pipeline/MANIFEST.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Requires the documented host/toolchain and project setup; seatbelt-only mode has no such complete delivery loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent builder edits and conflicts with unmerged work. - Distinct S1 units: separate feature/subtask builder cells in parallel-wave mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two cells write the same planned code file or concurrently commit the shared working tree. - Attenuating coordination relation: pairwiseDisjoint forces overlapping waves to run sequentially; disjoint workers do not commit, and a single integrator commits the wave. feature-auto also returns overlaps in-flight branch before build. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: overlap changes execution from parallel to serial, or stops a feature until the branch is merged/restarted; each later wave observes integrated predecessor state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the dispatch decision explicitly tests the actual shared-file collision surface and constrains subsequent writes/commits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concrete cross-builder file/commit interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the prescribed safe wave/stop response to intersecting file scopes. - Decision owner: developer-authored collision rule and configured override; the overlap agent gathers intersections rather than owning discretionary organizational settlement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task graph, file sets, sequential/parallel executor, worktree and integrator role. - Closure path: planned file intersection → serialize/stop → constrained later builder operation. - Boundary reachability: the installed feature-core pipeline mode intentionally exposes this function-specific path; session mode is a single writer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fixed rule is not autonomous coordination discretion; a function-specific path exists, but autonomous coordination authority must still be composed. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-auto.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: C is limited to evidenced file/commit interference, not semantic coordination or generic task sequencing.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the project&#39;s current outstanding commitments on behalf of the whole. - Whole-system current view: triage facts/report account for open and in-progress tasks, stale work, main-branch completion evidence, queue limit and fresh tasks excluded from detailed review. - Current-control decision scope: retire obsolete/completed/unsupported commitments or defer work; retain exceptions selected by the project owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unbounded accumulated task commitments and stale work competing with present delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which proposed close/defer actions become authoritative. - Decision owner: legitimate local project owner answering the Remote Control queue-review session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: timer/fleet, read-only product-owner model proposal, numbered report/TTL, choose parser and bd CLI. - Closure path: project queue facts → proposal/report to owner → yes/except/number selection → triage.py apply executes only selected close/defer decisions → later queue-driven work changes. - Boundary reachability: fleet provisions the first-party triage path for a project with bd; the same shipped script opens the owner-visible session and implements apply. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model recommends; no commitment is closed without the owner&#39;s returned answer. Static agent/credit caps are enforcement, not an additional A S3 mode. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/scripts/triage.py; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/fleet.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Only the configured local project queue is covered; no claim of autonomous whole-fleet management or cryptographic human authentication.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge builder claims of a correct, appropriately tested implementation through complementary source inspection. - Claim being audited: the builder&#39;s completed/tested branch respects requirements, project invariants and test-layer contracts. - Ordinary reporting path: builder result and green scoped/full gates. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer reads the actual branch diff, neighboring source and standards through test/invariant/simplify lenses. - Independence boundary: separately instantiated read-only reviewer role, separate context, no authority to alter its target or decide merge; it can report defects despite builder/gate success. - Who acts on findings: a separate fix builder verifies/rejects scoped findings with reasons, edits and reruns gates; unresolved critical findings prevent a clean result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-reporting blind spots, omitted tests, invariant breaches and false clean claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge evidence-backed findings against raw source and the stated contract. - Decision owner: fresh first-party reviewer agent; fix/triage authority is separately owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role tools/prompt, finding schemas, severity filtering and workflow outcome gate. - Closure path: raw diff inspection → structured findings → bounded fix or justified rejection → tests/commit and blockersRemaining/error result. - Boundary reachability: feature-core invokes shipped reviewer role after build, and passes findings into its shipped Fix stage; the manifest packages this into the supported feature command. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent audit judgment is model-owned; schema parsing and gates only transport/enforce it. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/agents/reviewer.md; plugins/dark-factory/templates/base/docs/review-contract.md; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Independence is contextual and role-based, not separate infrastructure or empirical proof of defect detection.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt the delivery factory&#39;s available organizational capability as its project environment changes. - External distinction: level inspection detects deployment/production configuration and growth to multiple human contributors; module manifests distinguish client production and monitoring requirements. - Future / prospective distinction: those signals motivate future unattended operation, client support/monitoring or a changed team-control arrangement, rather than another requested product fix. - Adaptation option generated: level report proposes the next structural step and its consequences; shipped add/provision paths identify concrete capability modules and their prerequisites. - Path back into current capability / S3: owner invokes the selected add/provision skill; file installation, seam adaptation, tests and status checks make the new module available to subsequent project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a factory outgrowing its supported operating arrangement as production/client/team demands change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select whether and how the proposed structural capability is adopted. - Decision owner: project owner; the level/add skills explicitly reserve the selection and structural step to that owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: level.py signals/report/hint, module manifest, installer, project checks and fleet provisioning. - Closure path: environmental evidence → report/options to owner → explicit chosen structural command → shipped module installation/configuration/test/status → changed current factory capability. - Boundary reachability: level SessionStart hint and explicitly invoked level/add commands ship with the plugin; installer copies real modules, not future roadmap placeholders. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent chooses adaptation; neither deterministic hints nor a model implementing the chosen installation owns the adoption decision. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/scripts/level.py; plugins/dark-factory/skills/level/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/skills/add/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/add-module.sh; plugins/dark-factory/hooks/hooks.json. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, bounded to the documented operator-led installation/return path. - Caveats: No autonomous S4 or empirical adaptation outcome is claimed. Vulnerability repair of a customer product and ordinary feature planning remain S1, not the credited S4 witness.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close changes to the delivery factory&#39;s ultimate delegation/review policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether the project permits solo/standard/team delivery authority, including mandatory human approval and whether agents may weaken governing fences. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the local project owner using the explicitly human-invoked level command; no agent-owned alternative is supplied. - Return-to-operation path: UserPromptSubmit records an exact project/target-bound expiring one-use grant; level.py applies the authorized fence change; guard reads the stricter working/main fence so weakening binds only after integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension between delivery freedom/unattended work and the project&#39;s reserved governance constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: authorize a change to the standing governance fence, rather than approve an individual operational command. - Decision owner: legitimate project owner; unattended/model-launched attempts cannot create the first-party prompt grant. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: level skill, prompt hook, grant store/TTL, atomic fence update, protected merge path and PreToolUse guard. - Closure path: policy-change proposal → owner explicitly names target and confirms → exact grant consumed → effective fence changed → later delivery/merge actions governed by returned policy. - Boundary reachability: level and guard are installed plugin hooks; the explicit level skill is disabled for model invocation and is the documented policy-change route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human authority decides the standing policy. A hook denial, ordinary release acceptance or permission dialog alone is not this S5 witness. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/skills/level/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/level.py; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/guard.py; plugins/dark-factory/hooks/hooks.json. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the implemented parent policy route. - Caveats: This is local configured-project ultimate policy, not authority over the host/provider or the OSS organization. Hooks are explicitly not a complete security sandbox.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/66623e14b849320d16f382ccf97a21476119d081/assessments/da/dark-factory.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 66623e14b849320d16f382ccf97a21476119d081\">66623e1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:36:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MUST-Company-AX-Booster/Open-Agent-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MUST-Company-AX-Booster/Open-Agent-Harness/tree/2b17fda4b5f0fe55dd577913aad8bd5febd4ba92\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b17fda4b5f0fe55dd577913aad8bd5febd4ba92\">2b17fda &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Answer team/project queries, interpret evidence and optionally draft/execute authorized Jira changes through a model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous messages, changing project facts, incomplete histories, source inconsistencies, connector responses and failed searches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose relevant sources and search queries, reconcile newer versus older facts, decide whether the requested response or a next lookup is needed. - Decision owner: Model-bearing Hermes Agent session, using first-party team-history skill and linked tool responses. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned Hermes tool loop, teamdb/Jira connectors, collector, project instructions, permission and write gates. - Closure path: User question → Hermes model selects a source/tool → reads raw history and task evidence → revises query/reasoning → returns cited answer or asks permission for an action. - Boundary reachability: Compose actually starts hermes gateway run with configured model and mounts SOUL.md plus team-history skill; pinned Hermes is a checked-in gitlink built by the standard distribution, not a hypothetical provider API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent selects which evidence matters and the response/action; a database, polling loop and optional model selector cannot make those semantic choices. - Evidence: Compose, team-history decisions/tool search, pinned Hermes agent entry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the standard model/tool composition; no external Slack/API-key live replay was run. - Caveats: A collector or teamdb server alone cannot own the task operation. S1 depends on a user-configured model provider and source access.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately owned regulation of mutual interference among multiple independent operational cells is established in the shipped Open Agent deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential competing subagent actions and shared sources are limited by tool scopes, provider caps and simple request order rather than an active peer-correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect conflicting commitments of separately acting units and feed a corrective coordination choice to them; not established. - Decision owner: Hermes parent may launch independent research children, but they return results directly without a shipped inter-child conflict adjudication relation; fixed tool/session caps are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation.maxconcurrentchildren, subagent iteration caps, platform toolsets, read-only shared history and allowed SQL writes. - Closure path: Child results return to the lead and are synthesized as a single answer (S3); no separate inter-child collision detection → coordination decision → revised commitments is traced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead receiving parallel facts is not evidence that agents regulate interference among their work, and six mascot roles are expressly decorative. - Evidence: README mascot and architecture boundary, delegation caps/toolsets, router&#39;s bounded research parallelism. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the configured Open Agent distribution. - Caveats: Upstream Hermes may support additional collaboration modes, but they are not credited unless installed and reachable in this project&#39;s actual distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: For one complex user request, the lead Hermes model sees the full requested outcome, permitted sources, Todo plan, available helper results and current clarification/Jira approval status. - Current-control decision scope: Split multi-source research into independent agent tasks, prioritize searches, choose whether further evidence is needed, and integrate subordinate findings into one owner-facing project answer. - Function: Organize current cross-source and delegated research commitments for a whole bounded user task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple overlapping sources, limited child budget, insufficient evidence, conflicting status claims and slow tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose 3–6 Todo steps, which independent research tasks merit parallel delegation, wait for helper returns, and change next evidence requests or answer based on combined findings. - Decision owner: Lead Hermes model (not Jev classifier); user retains permission to approve externally visible Jira mutations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: oarouter complex-task guidance, pinned Hermes Todo/delegation tools, read-only search, child caps, Jira write approval and project charter. - Closure path: Whole question and available sources → model chooses subtask/resource allocation → parallel agents and tools return evidence → lead decides what still needs verification and whether to deliver, update plan or request consent. - Boundary reachability: delegation is explicitly in the Slack platform toolset, and the model-facing complex-task PLAN is supplied in the documented configured Jev mode; ordinary Hermes delegation remains available independently of that optional selector. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses work partition and integration; the router merely classifies message complexity/model tier, and the three-child cap only limits concurrency. - Evidence: model-facing PLAN and routing hook, enabled delegation and limits, pinned Hermes delegation lifecycle. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; functionality is source-visible, but a full multi-agent Slack session was not replayed. - Caveats: A single trivial lookup has no active S3 team-control demand. An unconfigured Jev ladder does not supply a model-routing decision, but does not remove Hermes&#39;s normal agent/delegation path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped independently owned complementary audit of the lead/worker&#39;s task claims with distinct evidence and corrective return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False project status answers and unreliable AI summaries; accuracy guidance requests multiple sources but does not create a separate audit organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a producer&#39;s claims using raw primary evidence and return corrective findings to the controller; no distinct role or governed path installed. - Decision owner: The same Hermes assistant gathers, cross-checks and answers facts; user confirmations and SQL constraints do not decide an independent audit verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source citations, team-history chronological checks, Jira status lookups and nightly summaries. - Closure path: Same agent re-searches evidence before replying; no audit actor independent of producer or guaranteed corrective channel from a separate verifier. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reading multiple sources is normal S1 answer verification, and delegated research facts are not automatically an independently audited production claim. - Evidence: SOUL accuracy rules, history search procedure, config toolsets. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped deployment. - Caveats: An independently configured reviewer could be added to Hermes outside this deployment; that is not established by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental scanning that autonomously changes the assistant&#39;s future operating repertoire is demonstrated in the actual configured modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New team discussions and changing project facts are retained as summaries but not converted into future capability/strategy updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a revised future operational strategy or skill configuration from external change and return it into future task control; not established. - Decision owner: Nightly Hermes model creates a retrospective channel summary, while a human configures integrations and project identity; neither is shown deciding and applying future operating adaptation in this distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: summaries table, team-history search, Cron trigger and model-selected tools for current queries. - Closure path: Yesterday&#39;s messages → model summary → next query may retrieve facts; no generated adaptation option changes the future monitoring or agent capability policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Storage/search that improves future factual recall is not itself an S4 outside-and-then adaptation decision. Optional Jev per-message model routing optimizes current question processing, not strategic capability learning. - Evidence: nightly summaries prompt, team-history retrieval, router runtime. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high within pinned deployed modes. - Caveats: Teams may manually edit future tasks after reading summaries; that would require separately established adaptation ownership/return to change this state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What the self-hosted Open Agent is for, whose project it serves, which product/team terms it adopts and which standing source/action policies bind its replies. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Legitimate installation/project owner edits the mounted project SOUL.md and chooses integration/authorization configuration during setup; the model cannot authoritatively rewrite that read-only-mounted identity file through its disabled file/terminal tools. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes project charter in hermes/SOUL.md → Compose mounts it into Hermes&#39;s home as read-only → pinned Hermes loadsoulmd inserts its content into subsequent system context → future agent replies and source choices observe the owner&#39;s identity/policy. - Function: Maintain the assistant&#39;s local project-team purpose and binding editorial/source boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes to team identity, authoritative project sources and enduring desired response/governance rules across tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Define/replace the durable team-agent purpose and source policy, independent of one current lookup. - Decision owner: Installation owner / authorized project maintainer, not agent subtask router or external model provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Setup skill, editable SOUL.md, read-only volume mount, Hermes first-party identity loader, platform toolsets. - Closure path: Parent decides a policy/identity change → edits persistent charter and restarts/reloads as needed → Hermes assembles revised model prompt → subsequent operational decisions follow it. - Boundary reachability: The standard Compose mounts this exact SOUL file read-only and the pinned source at gitlink f97608f loads it as primary identity; no optional outside reviewer or local test-only code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The human changes binding project identity; the model uses it but cannot give itself that ultimate authority through ordinary Slack tool calls. - Evidence: project SOUL and About-project block, setup instructions, read-only mount and tool denial, pinned Hermes SOUL loader. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for local parent-authorized charter return, with no provider-backed behavioral measurement. - Caveats: S5 parent mode is the durable project identity, not generic API-key setup, ticket approval or maintainer GitHub governance. Installing a third-party skill cannot authorize contrary identity changes.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d02e14f075b317d16659690f3afc091eb433623/assessments/mu/must-company-ax-booster-open-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d02e14f075b317d16659690f3afc091eb433623\">6d02e14 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:10:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OPAL</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal/tree/e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\">e999c21 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the training-optimizer task through model-chosen executable updater/analysis behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Loss/gradient dynamics, parameter roles, code failures and the operator&#39;s fixed training goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Keep or edit updater/analysis programs and choose the corresponding next optimization behavior. - Decision owner: The model-backed AgentWorker decision actor in supported live backends. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent backend session, decision workspace, syntax/closure checks, program loader, update-boundary installation, DDP transport, trial snapshots and rollback. - Closure path: Agent edits are collected from the workspace, checked, installed as active programs and executed on subsequent updates; actual outcomes/errors and adoption status are exported into later decisions. - Boundary reachability: wrap instantiates the first-party worker/backend and supplies its prompt/workspace; apply and install make completed choices active. The repository owns the observation-to-decision-to-training organization even though a coding CLI supplies the actor runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves the unchanged starting optimizer or keep backend and deterministic safeguards; the discretionary program choices do not occur. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/backends/init.py; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate S2 inter-operational-unit stabilization organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential rank disagreement and stale asynchronous program answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: No first-party function-specific decisive owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DDP synchronization, agree/checkranks, one worker in flight and file-level base merge. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ranks/parameter groups are replicas or parts of one logical training operation. Version agreement and stale-answer merge preserve one optimizer&#39;s consistency; they do not establish a regulator of interference among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/misc/ddp.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current-control function at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Decision spending, waiting time, current training constraints and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: The operator selects fixed constraints; the agent owns bounded optimizer decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budgetleft/costreached, schedule/waiting policy, watchdog, program adoption and recovery. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses local optimizer behavior; the wrapper enforces host-selected budget, horizon and safety rules. No whole-system organizational resource/commitment bargaining or intervention mandate is supplied. Parameter-wise learning rates remain task algorithm choices, not S3 merely because they allocate step sizes. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/schedule.py; opal/misc/waiting.py; opal/backends/base.py; opal/prompts/neutral.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Provide complementary evidence challenging whether evaluation measurements reflect lasting training progress rather than temporary parameter substitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An updater can move weights for a validation measurement and restore them afterward, misleading ordinary reported performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect/report suspicious parameter round trips and enable an independently mandated reviewer/controller to judge their significance and corrective action. - Decision owner: The shipped Integrity sampler/analyzer deterministically owns diagnostic flags; an autonomous investigating audit actor and corrective mandate still require composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Seeded tensor samples/ring buffers, validation-opened windows, versioned evalintegrity events, stderr warnings and HTML report banner. - Closure path: Flags reach operator-facing events/report and direct the operator to inspect programs before trusting metrics. Automatic intervention and an autonomous review-to-control return are not shipped; this is the remaining constructor obligation. - Boundary reachability: With validation=N, evalintegrity defaults on in the real wrapper. reportvalidation opens a window and per-update recording resolves it; report.integrityflag consumes the resulting events. This is runtime code rather than only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source supplies an audit-specific independent reality-access and diagnostic/report path, rather than a generic log hook. It does not supply agent-owned audit judgment or correction, so C rather than A is credited. - Evidence: opal/misc/integrity.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/report.py; tests/testevalintegrity.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded constructor claim. - Caveats: The agent is not told of the check, but generated Python is not proven unable to evade it. Flags do not stop/revert training; warnings alone are not completed autonomous audit closure. - Claim being audited: That the observed validation performance describes the durably trained model produced by the active updater. - Ordinary reporting path: Generated analysis reports and scalar validation measurements delivered to the optimization worker. - Complementary access path: Wrapper-owned sampling of actual parameter entries before/after externally reported validation, independent of agent-written analysis.py. - Independence boundary: The sampler and flag/report code are outside the generated programs and hidden from the agent prompt; testtheagentisnottold corroborates the documented separation. This is bounded access separation, not a security proof. - Who acts on findings: The operator is prompted to inspect saved programs. An application must compose an independent audit actor/authority and returned corrective control to close autonomous enactment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Develop and adopt prospective optimizer/observation capability changes from the training environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing loss/gradient behavior, validation-versus-training divergence, performance cost and the remaining future update horizon. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret the observed trajectory, form a predicted adaptation option, keep or rewrite the active optimizer/analysis, and revise that option after its consequences. - Decision owner: The persistent model-backed decision worker owns the bounded adaptation choice; the operator fixes the larger training identity and constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Training observations/inbox, versioned programs, syntax/build checks, apply/install, trial/fallback machinery and prior-outcome messages. - Closure path: The prompt requires predicted effect and falsification timing; AgentWorker passes current evidence and prior outcomes to the model. Completed option files become the active updater/analysis at a boundary. Their subsequent training effects/reversions return in the next request and prediction comparison. - Boundary reachability: This loop is wired by wrap/AgentWorker in the standard live backend mode; submit exports losses/reports/horizon, apply/install execute adopted files, and formatmessage/previousanswer report consequences. No user-written adaptation controller or adjacent research agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime can enforce a cap or reject code, but cannot select the prospective algorithmic response without the model. The same actor may perform S1 work and S4 adaptation; implementation co-location does not erase the distinct environment-to-capability role. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/agent.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared optimizer-run boundary. - Caveats: This is within-run adaptation of deployed optimizer capability. Merely writing code or updating memory would not suffice; the credited evidence includes explicit future prediction, executable adoption and subsequent environmental feedback. No empirical improvement or cross-task transfer is established. - External distinction: Actual training-workload loss/gradient/validation and latency distinctions arrive from outside the optimizer decision cell; the operator-fixed model/data/loss are its environment. - Future / prospective distinction: totalupdates and remaining decisions constrain the horizon; the prompt requires a predicted effect, by when, and what would falsify it, then comparison with observed outcomes. - Adaptation option generated: A kept or revised updater.py/analysis.py (and helper modules), changing the live update rule and/or the observation capability. - Path back into current capability / S3: wrapper.apply checks/merges the option and install replaces the active runtime objects; later updates use them and submit returns their consequences. This is a capability-return path, without claiming a separately agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy adjudication path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Proposals to change training purpose, data/model/loss or permitted optimizer scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer own the initial training purpose and constraints; no first-party runtime S5 organization is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static prompt/spec, backend workspace policy, code diagnostics and precision contracts. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator defines the goal and immutable training boundaries in advance. Ordinary notes, cost approval and code boundary rules do not provide an identity-level issue referral, legitimate authoritative resolution and returned governance loop. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/misc/boundary.py; opal/wrapper.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf/assessments/op/opal.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf\">5222205 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:36:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/supernovae/SwarmOS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwarmOS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/supernovae/SwarmOS/tree/c85010787b001981beb84e60502c1eaca3c6f812\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c85010787b001981beb84e60502c1eaca3c6f812\">c850107 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously interpret approved task acceptance criteria and produce/revise code, plans, tests and evidence for an owned WorkUnit. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unpredictable repository content, technical uncertainty, tool results, compile/test failures and defects within the approved task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive plan, implementation, and corrective tool/code steps in response to observed workspace/test feedback. - Decision owner: the configured managed model-driven planner/developer/tester agent in a first-party launched role, not the Git queue or lease manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompts, task bundle, native CLI adapters, isolated worktree, checkpoints, approved-base policy, claim fencing and report import. - Closure path: issue contract and approved base → launched role receives source/task context → model-driven work and tool actions → tests/environment feedback → changed artifact and implementation handoff or repair. - Boundary reachability: internal/workflow invokes configured role runners for approved WorkUnits in the shipped swarm issue work path, with native Codex and other qualified adapters; no repository-development-only actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: after removing the model-driven worker, CAS/validation/PR services still enforce custody but cannot choose the task-specific implementation; only supported managed workers supply that discretion. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/WORKFLOWS.md, internal/workflow/executeimplementation.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reachability and role ownership, not for success on every provider/runtime combination. - Caveats: individual provider model/tool loops are external dependencies; their unrelated S2–S5 mechanisms are excluded. Some platform/provider qualification is explicitly pending.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between separate workers claiming or publishing the same issue state and return the conflict result to later worker behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous worker claims, stale renewal/checkpoint publication, and one worker overwriting another&#39;s accepted state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept exactly one current claim under a fencing token, reject an old claimant, and cancel or prevent publication from the superseded attempt. - Decision owner: constructor-defined CAS/lease policy executed by SwarmOS rather than an autonomous coordination agent choosing its own exception policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git compare-and-swap refs, claimid, TTL, heartbeat, joint issue/checkpoint update, worktree isolation. - Closure path: rival claims or changed Git state → one fenced CAS succeeds and the other fails → stale worker is canceled/refused and must reacquire or hand off before subsequent work/publishing. - Boundary reachability: the claim/store implementation is mandatory for managed issue attempts in supported local/GitHub ledger modes, and subsequent artifact publication revalidates its claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the inter-worker conflict is real and transactionally closed, but the decisive resolution rule is prewritten, so coordination is constructor-controlled rather than an S2 agent discretion. - Distinct S1 units: separate concurrent managed coding-agent worker attempts/instances assigned against a shared issue/repository work organization. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate claim/overwrite or a stale worker publishing a checkpoint after another acquired the issue. - Attenuating coordination relation: unique fenced owner and CAS replacement rule, with cancellation/refusal on lease loss. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected/stale worker cannot continue or publish; subsequent work must use the current claim/checkpoint or be stopped. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly prevents a concrete clash of otherwise independent operational workers and returns the prevention to their later execution capability, not merely storing a task list. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, internal/coordination/store.go, internal/coordination/revoke.go, internal/workflow/executerun.go. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a narrow collision-attenuation path, not proof of a broad adaptive peer-negotiation metasystem.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate present task commitments, release/stop conditions and required verification for the approved WorkUnit across planner, developer, tester and publication stages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unapproved plan changes, stale workers, overrun repair loops, untrusted candidate changes to validation policy, and reported completion before required checks pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: withhold implementation or submit readiness when approved digests/checks/claim evidence disagree, reserve bounded repairs, and permit handoff/continuation only after policy-conforming current state. - Decision owner: constructor policy and authorized human WorkUnit/gate settings; managed agent roles produce artifacts but do not override approval, claim, or baseline validation predicates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkUnit digest and approved base SHA, phase machine, lease manager, protected-path checker, trusted-base validation specification and repair attempt budget. - Closure path: whole-issue current phase/artifact/claim/check results → deterministic admit, pause, retry, reject or finish transition → subsequent worker role and PR stage follows that decision. - Boundary reachability: internal/workflow/executerun.go, executevalidation.go and internal/pr/service.go govern every standard managed issue execution and publication path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the present-work control function is real but its decisive stage/authority rules are authored and enforced by SwarmOS policy; a model cannot autonomously waive them. - Whole-system current view: approved work objective, policy/base digests, lease/owner, phase, reports, checkpoint, required-check outcomes and repair budget for one WorkUnit. - Current-control decision scope: work admission, stage continuation, checkpoint trust, bounded retry, and readiness for an explicit submission; not a universal multi-project portfolio budget. - Evidence: pinned internal/contract/types.go, internal/workflow/executerun.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go, internal/pr/service.go, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for a bounded issue-level S3 constructor path. - Caveats: most approval and policy choices are parent or developer-owned; S3=C does not promote static enforcement to autonomous S3=A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementer/plan author&#39;s own report against independent source/acceptance evidence and return findings into corrective work before continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-reported correctness, incomplete acceptance coverage or a plausible implementation passing only its author&#39;s own checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately invoked reviewer/tester model assesses an exact plan/candidate against independent artifact/source/check evidence and produces change requests or failure findings. - Decision owner: the configured distinct reviewer/tester model-driven role; deterministic parsing, validation enforcement, and report import do not own the model&#39;s challenge judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: snapshot-isolated read-only review, output-only REVIEW.md, separate VALIDATION.md, trusted-base checks, role/provenance separation, bounded repair routing. - Closure path: author plan or candidate + self-report → separate reviewer/tester inspects source/acceptance and records findings → verdict/failing check blocks continuation → developer corrects candidate and the relevant check repeats. - Boundary reachability: the shipped agent review gate runs a configured reviewer in an isolated snapshot, while the standard validation phase invokes a separate tester and routes failed acceptance/check outcomes into bounded developer repair. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an independently invoked role receives the actual artifact and makes the substantive challenge, whereas SwarmOS&#39;s gate merely enforces its outcome. The positive claim is limited to modes with a distinct configured reviewer/tester. - Claim being audited: that a proposed plan or implemented candidate satisfies the approved scope/acceptance and can safely progress. - Ordinary reporting path: planner&#39;s plan/test plan or developer&#39;s implementation report and claimed commands/results. - Complementary access path: read-only isolated review of source/plan and separate tester&#39;s direct acceptance/required-check execution rather than only trusting the prior handoff. - Independence boundary: reviewer is isolated from modification of the author&#39;s worktree, and the tester is a separately invoked role with acceptance checks; the model&#39;s actual provider can be configured, so independence is actor/process and evidence-path, not automatically different model vendors. - Who acts on findings: SwarmOS stops promotion and returns failed validation or requested changes to the developer/plan path for bounded correction and recheck. - Evidence: pinned internal/agent/review.go, internal/workflow/executereview.go, internal/workflow/executevalidation.go, docs/WORKFLOWS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; independence is deployment/role-configuration-sensitive. - Caveats: static required checks alone are routine validation, not the S3 witness. Human-only review is not counted as autonomous S3; the cited distinct model-driven reviewer/tester mode is required.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped first-party future-capability adaptation loop over the SwarmOS work organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: issue backlog changes, new requests and tool/runtime upgrades are operational inputs, but not a demonstrated environment-model → prospective adaptation-option → changed capability loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users author new WorkUnits, repository maintainers choose product changes, and current-work agents choose implementation within a given contract. - Decision owner: external user/maintainer for future capability and present S1 roles for bounded tasks; no separately closed SwarmOS S4 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: issue triage, backlog scan, role guidance, diagnostic reports, roadmap and release process. - Closure path: incoming issue or planned product change enters an ordinary approved WorkUnit; no first-party S4 actor is shown returning a prospective environment-derived option into durable operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sensing a new issue, repairing a failing current PR and storing lessons are not by themselves outside-and-then capability adaptation. - Evidence: pinned docs/WORKFLOWS.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, ROADMAP.md, internal/workflow/backloglanes.go. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a parent organization using SwarmOS could run an S4 practice outside this distribution; it is not imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internally or parent-closed ultimate organizational identity/policy decision loop is established for a SwarmOS installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task constraints, protected paths, authentication and repository identity are set from outside as fixed policy/permissions, not deliberated by a first-party ultimate-policy authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintainer authorizes a task/plan and repository policy, but no shipped runtime path adjudicates a constitutional identity question and returns that ruling to govern the organization. - Decision owner: external repository maintainer/provider authorization for configuration and approvals; ordinary task actors operate inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable approval bindings, permission checks, WorkUnit policy, human gate, sandbox rules and versioned base SHA. - Closure path: configured policy is enforced for work; no identity/policy dispute → legitimate S5 adjudication → returned authoritative new identity/ultimate-policy state is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approvals and static permission rules are concrete authority constraints, but they do not close S5 by themselves. - Evidence: pinned internal/contract/types.go, docs/project.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, internal/lease/. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a larger OpenSiro- or company-like parent organization may possess S5 externally; no such authority is credited to the shipped SwarmOS installation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/12dcd070e444e2d0246446e5e65421f725c294d6/assessments/sw/swarmos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 12dcd070e444e2d0246446e5e65421f725c294d6\">12dcd07 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:17:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RSI-Flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/quqxui/RSI-Flow/tree/0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f87c2b198f22e5697853cfc6d0bf2d002e48ca7\">0f87c2b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A configured Pi/DeepSeek agent performs the substantive target task inside a first-party versioned workflow session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task instructions, tool/workspace state, model observations and failed subtasks under an operator-defined experiment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses its response/action within an exposed task turn; results and tool feedback inform subsequent turns where the selected SDK supports them. - Decision owner: The model-backed target agent operating through the native driver; the engine and experiment runner do not choose its per-task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native SDK bridge, workspace/runtime snapshots, bounded prompt interface, execution timeout, credentials, receipts and result parsing. - Closure path: Input task and versioned target artifact → native model-backed agent turn → task/tool result → next agent turn and final response/experiment feedback. - Boundary reachability: Native integration is shipped under integrations/native/, wired to Pi/DeepSeek adapters and documented as supported task execution, rather than only a test mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides task behavior; removing the native model leaves an experiment runner capable of testing artifacts, not autonomous task judgment. - Evidence: native worker, Pi driver, DeepSeek driver, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Task-level S1 depends on the configured native agent/provider; built-in offline demonstration is not proof of model autonomy or real-provider success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party anti-interference coordination among distinct task-performing S1 cells is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate trials could contend for resources or contaminate comparisons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The framework bounds parallel work and separates trial artifacts; no inter-S1 conflict-specific adjustment is evidenced. - Decision owner: Preselected execution protocol and deterministic trial harness rather than an autonomous coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task batching, trial directories, stable task IDs, budget reservations and timeouts. - Closure path: Runner results are gathered into comparison; no disturbance-detecting cross-agent coordination decision returns to alter their working relationships. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Isolation and scheduling protect tests but are not the S2-specific regulation of interactions between ongoing autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: validation stage, budget ledger, native runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Different target frameworks may implement their own coordination; those internals are outside this review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No agent-owned or specifically parent-governed whole-system discretionary control of concurrent current S1 priorities and commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget exhaustion, failed trials, protocol violations, incomplete receipts and target-version conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Acceptance thresholds and stop/rollback predicates are fixed by operator-selected protocol; these do not choose new organizational priorities in a current whole-system view. - Decision owner: Human configuration supplies constraints, deterministic engine enforces them; no in-run S3 discretion is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetLedger, SingleRoundSearch, decide, deploy, pause/resume/cancel/recover and process isolation. - Closure path: Status and budget checks deterministically stop, reject or deploy a version; the engine does not revise commitments among distinct ongoing S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An operational gate and rollback authority do not become managerial judgment merely because they have effect on execution. - Evidence: engine, selection, adapters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A custom SearchPolicy may implement additional control but no such standard autonomous whole-system owner is wired.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S1 claims with an audit-owned corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False task success, inaccurate version-loaded claims and unverifiable experiments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluator, outcome receipts and load verifier check protocol predicates but do not exercise separate complementary organizational audit judgment. - Decision owner: The same experiment engine owns ordinary comparison/admission; no independently authorized auditor is composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Paired evaluation, task receipts, exact-version digest, load verification, report evidence and regression thresholds. - Closure path: Comparison flows into ordinary Stage 6 acceptance; failed load checks trigger procedural rollback, not an independent audit finding sent to a corrective S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Test comparison and defensive proof of load are valuable controls, but routine QA is not necessarily an independent complementary S3 challenge. - Evidence: validation, selection, reports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: An operator can independently inspect raw receipts, but that is not an evidenced first-party audit-return mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: A configured reasoning agent generates prospective changes to the target agent&#39;s prompt/workflow from accumulated experience and diagnosed limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring task failures, observed regressions, changes in future task mix and limits of the deployed artifact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose candidate artifact changes, hypothesis and expected benefit, or explicitly propose no change; a subsequent protocol governs acceptance. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed CommandProposer (including the Codex integration) chooses a future adaptation option; the human-proposal mode separately supplies offline input but does not erase the supported autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured diagnosis, proposal context, key allowlist, versioned artifact build, baseline/candidate comparison, decide and verified deploy. - Closure path: Historical experience → diagnosis → reasoning backend chooses prospective artifact modifications → constrained build and paired evaluation → accepted new deployed artifact changes later target-agent sessions. - Boundary reachability: Engine.proposer selects command reasoning from config and Stage 4 invokes proposer.propose; the returned candidate flows into supported Stage 5 and Stage 6 deployment, not merely into documentation. - External distinction: Imported version-labelled task outcomes and feedback characterize target performance beyond the current agent turn. - Future / prospective distinction: Proposed prompt/workflow modifications concern future agent capability rather than a response to a present task. - Adaptation option generated: A structured candidate with hypothesis, expectedbenefit and allowed artifact changes, or nochange. - Path back into current capability / S3: The accepted content-addressed version is loaded/verified for subsequent task runs, with rollback after unsuccessful deployment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses the substantive candidate hypothesis and changes; deterministic gates can reject it but do not invent the adaptation option. - Evidence: proposal reasoning, engine proposer wiring, improvement, deployment. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Autonomous adaptation requires configured reasoning backend and provider; a candidate is not deployed without human-selected test thresholds and successful tests. Source evidence is not a live improvement-outcome claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution and binding return for the RSI installation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: What targets, prompts, models, budgets and artifact fields should be legitimately allowed to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator fixes target schema, allowed keys, model configuration and evaluation rules at initialization; no identity-policy issue is referred to an adjudicator for resolution. - Decision owner: Operator/developer outside the live run; model proposer is restricted to editable lower-level artifact fields. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable config hash, key allowlist, model identity snapshots, validation errors and manual stop. - Closure path: Fixed policy blocks out-of-bounds proposals; it does not carry an identity question to a legitimate policy owner and feed a fresh ruling back into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Artifact edits and accepted versions adjust capability, not ultimate purpose/constitutional identity; immutable settings are enforcement not adjudication. - Evidence: definition, improvement, engine. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A developer may deliberately install new policies in a new workspace, but that is not a first-party governed S5 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822/assessments/rs/rsi-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 35a76015978b6753215beac6e12cb62e542ba822\">35a7601 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:32:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentForeman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman/tree/634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\">634f9c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce candidate code/experiments and general task artifacts through model decisions with returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, failed code/training, poor candidates and contradictory evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose experiment/repair/retest/stop and instructions; author source or artifacts; revise after independent criticism. - Decision owner: configured model-backed manager, coding worker, contributors and integrator, invoked by first-party protocols. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source snapshots, typed responses, worker pool leases, edit allowlist, native training/checks, archives and round/deadline bounds. - Closure path: verified baseline → manager model choice → worker action → independent measurement/criticism → next choice or revision → preserved terminal result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model roles leaves deterministic transport and checks but no substantive hypothesis, edit or report decision. - Caveats: A is structural ownership, not proof of live model quality; external coding tools are credited only as explicitly assembled task actors. - Boundary reachability: CLI campaign/overnight and cooperate/debate commands assemble these roles and execute their chosen actions; they are shipped supported modes, not test-only prompts. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/engine.py; src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/poolworker.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately closed inter-S1 interference regulation at the declared task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent contributions can be inconsistent; worker contention and duplicate execution are mechanically bounded. - Decisive decision or feedback right: integrator chooses combined output, but contributors are not subsequently coordinated through a specific inter-unit correction loop. - Decision owner: integrator owns S1 synthesis; runtime owns fixed sequencing/leases, with no separately evidenced autonomous S2 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent assignments, integration contract, queue/lease locks, quota cooldown and critique of combined artifacts. - Closure path: contributors return once → integrator revises the combined product; duplicate jobs are suppressed by fixed IDs/locks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition, waiting for all contributions and rewriting one combined answer do not establish mutual adjustment of operational units. - Caveats: does not deny coordination in a broader organization assembled around this tool. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py; docs/COOPERATIVE.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system discretionary current-control function over a portfolio established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate failure, incumbent comparison, quota waits and bounded task duration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager chooses the next experiment within one fixed objective; it cannot change resources, task goal or budgets. - Decision owner: model owns local S1 research choices; operator/configuration selects task scope, role pool and limits; runtime enforces those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign rounds/best record, budget/deadline checks, role registry, UI status, cancel and resume. - Closure path: measured rounds return to a next-task-action choice; generic stop/confirmation returns no evidenced whole-system resource bargain. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the campaign manager name does not turn its local experimental policy into a metasystem; fixed quotas are enforcement. - Caveats: broader multi-project allocation may be performed externally but is outside the declared first-party campaign mode. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge operational claims through separate model audit/critique with complementary original evidence and corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported synthesis claims, missed criticism, inadequate experiment plans and false candidate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct critic/auditor chooses anchored findings and accept/revise/reject; campaign reviewer can block or return an executable proposal for revision. - Decision owner: separately invoked model reviewer/auditor roles; deterministic gates apply verdict consistency and run trusted checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct role identities, read-only review snapshot, source/check/artifact anchors, issue ledger, frozen verifier and bounded revision/retest. - Closure path: raw artifacts/source/check receipts → separate critic judgment → integrator/manager revision and recheck, or stopped/needs-evidence/rejected result; council audit conditions final disposition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model audit judgment owns substantive criticism; deterministic parsing and trusted checkpoint measurement are support, not the owner of that judgment. - Caveats: distinct identities are not statistically independent models; supplied sources are not authenticated by quote matching, and live factual correctness remains unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: documented campaign --review, cooperative distinct-reviewer mode and council audit are shipped runtime modes; no developer fixture is needed to select them. - Claim being audited: candidate/proposal or chair/integrator output is supported by original source material, controls, actual artifacts and check results. - Ordinary reporting path: manager rationale, proposer artifacts and chair/integrator summary. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives copied source snapshots plus experiment evidence; general critics receive full artifact/source/check anchor documents, and council auditor sees original answers and all criticism rather than chair summary alone. - Independence boundary: separate read-only sessions/role identities from author/integrator/chair; no authorship permission for the reviewer. Trusted verifier directly reads tensors without importing candidate code, adding corroboration rather than autonomous judgment. - Who acts on findings: campaign manager/integrator revises under recorded issue IDs; controller blocks/returns or marks needsevidence/rejected instead of fabricating success. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/discussion.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/templates/moons/verify.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective adaptation loop changing AgentForeman operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new research hypotheses, failed experiment results, provider quota changes and user task uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager may change candidate experiment code, not harness capability/organization; task chair clarifies current requirements. - Decision owner: task agents own research outcomes; maintainer/operator owns external product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research plans, measurements, council sources, quota information and roadmap. - Closure path: research findings return to the current task; no prospective environmental judgment returns as a change to harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research as the primary operational transformation is S1; internal optimization and quota reaction do not establish S4. - Caveats: an operator could use outputs to redesign its organization, but that external act is not a shipped adaptation closure. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; docs/ROADMAP.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established beyond bounded task contracts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unclear scope, changed deliverables/acceptance/autonomy and stale user confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human confirms a task contract; agents must pause when they report a scope change, rather than decide ultimate policy. - Decision owner: operator owns the local task mandate; product identity remains maintainer-defined outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: version/fingerprint confirmation, allowed-decision/pause fields, frozen checks, scope-change signal and stop controls. - Closure path: briefing → exact human confirmation → bound child task; requested scope changes pause for new clarification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is operational task admission and authority bounding; it is not an identity/ultimate-policy dispute between present operations and future adaptation. - Caveats: contract editing is a real returned human decision but does not by itself satisfy the function-first S5 threshold. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/taskcontract.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; docs/BRIEFING.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801/assessments/ag/agentforeman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801\">8100f85 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness² CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli/tree/0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\">0c81134 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-backed improvement work per encountered task: examine operational evidence, edit a harness, probe the edited candidate and produce a selected task harness plus result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user problems and actual coding-agent failures demand different instruction, skill, script or hook changes; candidate edits can also violate a harness component contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured improver chooses candidate file changes and explains them against evidence. The model selector (in automatic mode) chooses a final task result where multiple candidates exist. - Decision owner: Model-backed proposer/editor and optional automatic selector, invoked through the first-party session interface. External executor cognition is a dependency, but the improvement goal and agent-edit control path are shipped by the reviewed CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace harvest, component allowlist, tree validation, per-stage retry/fallback, rollout adapter, result/trajectory records and candidate selection parser. - Closure path: Current base-task rollout → independent agent-edited harness trees → validated candidate task probes → composed candidate and selector result → accepted task-harness and evidence for later reuse. - Boundary reachability: cli.run constructs Proposer with the installed channel config and invokes schedule.runsweep; runtask calls recursion.runmode, whose runstage actually invokes Proposer.call with writable context/harness and captures its changed tree, then probes candidates. All are included in the shipped CLI mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes contentful harness edits and, when configured, evidence-driven task-result choices; source-local Python merely bounds and enforces those decisions. Dry-run, static tests and deterministic fixtures by themselves do not prove model autonomy. - Evidence: run.py, recursion.py, proposer.py, schedule.py. - Basis: Supported live-model session path at the frozen commit, not asserted empirical success rate. - Confidence: High for source-wired decision ownership; outcome quality untested. - Caveats: The target coding assistant and provider are externally configured and not extra first-party viable S1 units.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced S2-specific stabilization among two distinct, separately viable owned S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate and domain execution might consume resources, but distinct S1 divisions and an inter-unit oscillation/collision control relation are not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic concurrency width, domain ordering, semaphore and run-label isolation coordinate experimental steps, not an agentic inter-S1 conflict response. - Decision owner: Static Python scheduler and operator configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ThreadPoolExecutor, proposer semaphore, namespace isolation, mode/domain libraries and run budget. - Closure path: Scheduled experiments complete and aggregate; no S2-specific sensed interference and returned reciprocal adjustment across autonomous operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stage-level parallelism, composition and result pooling are parts of one bounded optimization process, not S2 because they run concurrently. - Evidence: schedule.py, recursion.py, proposer.py. - Basis: Scoped source negative after examining task and domain concurrency. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product using this CLI might provide S2 independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current control relationship over independently viable operational divisions at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget overruns, candidate failures, task completion, user approval and project file drift are local run/deployment constraints rather than multi-unit resource/commitment governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator sets execution budget, task set and allowed surface; runtime enforces limits; selectors compare results. None revises current commitments across distinct S1 units with systemic authority. - Decision owner: CLI user for configuration, deterministic scheduler for enforcement and model for candidate choice; no separately supported S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Budget/call caps, run status, concurrency limit, retries, apply conflict checks and --force option. - Closure path: Fixed run settings → bounded task experimentation → output; absent whole-current S3 view, discretionary priority/resource intervention and returned binding adjustment to multiple ongoing units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Picking a better harness is an operational or prospective adaptation choice; enforcing an operator&#39;s preset budget is not agent-owned S3 and ordinary apply approval is not parent-governed whole-system control. - Evidence: run.py, schedule.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of whole-organization current-control closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator may manage a larger organization, but its current control is not implemented by this application.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently complementary audit channel challenges routine S1–S3 reports and closes findings into corrective systemic control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad candidate harness files, inaccurate agent result claims and poor task answers are checked by local tree validation, rollout comparisons, benchmark judging or optional evaluation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Ordinary candidate validators, task selector and eval compare outputs; none has separately independent authority to audit a standing S3 report and route corrective findings to management. - Decision owner: Deterministic validators/evaluation routines, configured model selector or human comparator within the same adaptation process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harness component checks, anonymous candidate selection, result reports and user-triggered eval. - Closure path: Candidate/result evidence → within-loop selection and optional later quality measurement; no complementary audit → binding whole-current corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent improver proposals and blind selection reduce some bias, but are routine production/evaluation, not an independent S3 oversight path. - Evidence: selection.py, eval.py, judge.py. - Basis: Scoped negative distinguishing quality checks from complementary audit independence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate external QA team could supply such audit for a deployed agent but is outside this reviewed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model-backed across-task prospective harness adaptation with a second, explicitly supported parent-controlled blind-selection mode that changes which candidate and lesson enter the future task library. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar project tasks, newly observed errors and weak transfer of task-specific improvements to later tasks; user may also disagree with automated candidate choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Automatic selector chooses the adopted candidate and reflector extracts a transferable lesson; in --selector human mode the user personally selects among blinded final candidates while a model reflector can distill the adopted result. The choice feeds subsequent tasks through the library. - Decision owner: A: model-backed selector and reflector/proposer in CLI sweep; P: operator at the shipped interactive selector, not the benchmark author or external research institute. Each mode has separately attributable adoption authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-domain sequential Library, selectionpool, anonymous candidate files, human TTY prompt/choice, adopted tree and experience persistence, context.build injection into later proposer contexts; final project deployment is optional with apply. - Closure path: Encounters and candidate execution evidence → automatic/human chosen task harness → model-reflected generalizable lesson → modelib.add → next task&#39;s proposal context includes earlier domain library → potential changed subsequent harness. After a run, generated consolidated harness may also be explicitly applied to project files. - Boundary reachability: Shipped cli/run.py chooses HumanSelector when selector.channel.kind == human; otherwise it instantiates model-backed Proposer. Both invoke schedule.runsweep and selection.runselection; schedule.runonemode calls reflectadoption and conditionally modelib.add, while next task&#39;s runstage loads the library. cli/apply.py separately permits reviewed real project-file adoption. - External distinction: Actual newly encountered user task domains and contrasted coding-agent trajectories expose operational shortcomings not specified in a fixed harness. - Future / prospective distinction: Per-domain transferable lessons plus consolidated project harness target future tasks, rather than merely grading or repairing one immediate deliverable. - Adaptation option generated: Model editor proposes changed skills/instructions/scripts/hooks; model/human selector adopts a tested candidate; reflector writes future-reuse cues; consolidation proposes project-wide next-task harness. - Path back into current capability / S3: Selected experience is persisted in the per-domain library and read by subsequent model proposer sessions, changing the options considered for later edits. The optional apply command installs consolidated or one-task adopted components into the user&#39;s project; no independent S3 manager is asserted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A has agent-owned candidate choice and lesson generation with model decisions returning through first-party memory to later operations. The distinct P mode assigns the same consequential selection right to the user through a real terminal interaction and returns it through the same library. This does not assign the separately guarded final installation to the model. - Evidence: run.py, human.py, selection.py, schedule.py, library.py, apply.py. - Basis: The two first-party shipped prospective adaptation loops share the persistent future-facing library, but have distinct actual adoption decision owners. - Confidence: High for reachable branch/closure, medium for model generalization since no model runs were made. - Caveats: --selector human requires a TTY and multiple finalists (k &gt; 1); at k=1 selection is trivial. A failed reflection yields no transferable lesson. Project apply defaults to human approval and is not evidence of autonomous project deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate identity/mission-policy adjudication is shipped for the overall improvement organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operator task selection, allowed editable files, model/provider choice, benchmark scope and safety limits bound execution but do not create a separate ultimate policy decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses and configures the run and can approve/force project-file writes; no identity-level conflict is escalated to a legitimate authority and returned as governing policy. - Decision owner: External developer/project owner for the overall purpose; shipped runtime for validation and configured model for bounded edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI config and setup, component tree validator, source boundaries, apply confirmation and git-dirty checks. - Closure path: User-provided goal and constraints → local optimization and optional installation; absent system identity dispute → authority judgment → returned revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervised application is an operational/capability adoption gate, not by itself an identity- or ethos-level S5 decision. The parent-mode S4 classification does not automatically imply S5=P. - Evidence: settings.py, run.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative for identity-level authority and operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The user&#39;s broader software organization may have S5; this repository does not establish its governance authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335/assessments/ha/harness-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335\">b4570aa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T18:56:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LearnHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness/tree/3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\">3dc8847 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-directed teaching, research, demonstration construction and visual/PDF assistance under a chosen lesson mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain starting competence, unexpected wrong answers, unfamiliar facts, tool failures and domain-specific problems requiring outside evidence or visualization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Active prober/teacher models choose questions, explanation revisions, quiz checks, graph lookup and specialist delegation; specialist models choose searches, fetches, demo edits/tests and visual construction steps. - Decision owner: Shipped real Agent and runSubagent loops invoked through first-party models and tools; mere persistence/UI are not S1 decision owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-turn persisted reconstruction, probe-to-teach seam, schema-validated question/plan tools, model selection and BYOK gates, structured worker toolsets, source-specific job persistence and abort handling. - Closure path: Learner goal/response → prober or teacher model decides action → asks/teaches/checks, or dispatches a specialist model → learner answers or worker returns evidence/artifact → teacher uses new information in following action. - Boundary reachability: handleChatRequest constructs buildAgent for the prober/teacher and jobTools dispatches to real shipped subagent runners; explicit configured model keys are needed. Scripted test engines do not supply the credited autonomy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model agents select semantically different learning questions, explanation paths, search/fix iterations and specialist output; fixed question grading and JSON work queues alone cannot choose these. - Evidence: teacher runtime, job tool dispatch, researcher with web tools, demo builder with real headless check. - Basis: Shipped, model-backed adaptive operation and separately executable agent specialties. - Confidence: High source-path, medium live-provider efficacy (not executed). - Caveats: BYOK and required tool capabilities are deployment prerequisites. Tests use fake engines and do not validate pedagogical outcomes with real users.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a conflict/oscillation specifically between independent lesson S1 workers and feed a revised coordination commitment into those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing background job completions, shared graph writes, multiple specialist reports or two S1 units reaching incompatible conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Per-chat wake/steer deduplication and global graph audit lock serialize events; the teacher commissions specialists and receives their reports. No first-party autonomous inter-specialist disagreement negotiation or adjusted joint commitments is shown. - Decision owner: Deterministic job registry and semaphore control the mechanics; teacher handles the educational consequence as its S3/current-lesson decision, not a distinct inter-S1 S2 arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-chat serialized wake delivery and recovery, durable pending result IDs, global limited concurrency, source-local extraction single-flight and global serialized audit/merge. - Closure path: Queued worker completion is delivered to teacher once; two graph audits cannot concurrently read and write a stale graph. This is mechanical admission/stability without a distinctive semantic response returned to the conflicting S1 workers. - Boundary reachability: The named locks, duplicate IDs, consent gates and worker result steers are live first-party source paths, but no separate model mediator resolves S1-vs-S1 work collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Correct sequencing protects state, yet methodology requires specific inter-S1 disturbance, distinct discretionary response and returned adjustment of S1 behaviour, not delegation or a queue alone. - Evidence: serialized delivery / wake protocol, graph audit gate and source single-flight, six-system topology. - Basis: Bounded negative for agent-owned inter-S1 conflict attenuation; credible structural serialization is not enough. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can ask the teacher to reconcile contradictory reports; no dedicated shipped inter-S1 coordination mandate is thereby established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Keep one current learning session coherent across the learner&#39;s goal, assessed understanding, dependency plan, teaching steps and commissioned specialist work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Learner confusion invalidating current plan assumptions, wrong facts, delayed external support, mismatch between learning target and work in progress, specialist failures and changed user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The teacher has the current session transcript and goal, authors/revises the visible plan, decides which knowledge gap matters now, allocates research/demo/visual/PDF work and changes explanation/check sequence based on returned worker results and learner answers. - Decision owner: Model-bearing first-party teacher for the session-local teaching mission; learner can change the overall request, but deterministic planinit/queue enforcement is not the author of a new plan or delegation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prober handoff, current session record, DAG validator, question node tags, job input and result steers, durable wake, one-turn active registry, disabled-tool and model-role settings. - Closure path: Prober establishes starting point → teacher builds global lesson dependency tree → learner&#39;s answers or independent job results reveal a current gap → teacher revises explanation/plan and initiates/cancels/uses supporting work → next quiz/lesson state reflects that decision. - Boundary reachability: buildAgent(&quot;teacher&quot;) is attached after the real probedone handoff, has plan and job tool access in the supported chat pipeline; background worker result messages return to this same persistent session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: It is more than a blind router: the teacher decides a session-wide pedagogical priority and changes ongoing commitment to the goal in response to actual feedback. It does not manage the entire multi-session application, unrelated practice sets or a global provider budget. - Whole-system current view: Session transcript, learner goal, active plan/progress, commissioned worker result steers, and optional read-only graph inspection relevant to the session. - Current-control decision scope: Revise dependency plan/teaching sequence, task requests to multiple agents and response to produced evidence within the declared one-session recursion. - Evidence: teacher planning and on-the-fly repair, tool registry and model loop, worker feedback into live teacher, job instruction and correction. - Basis: Source-closed current session-wide semantic decision/return using autonomous teacher. Not a claim of product-wide S3 at higher recursion. - Confidence: Medium; stronger for declared one-session recursion than a broader multi-session organization. - Caveats: Plan progress tags are deterministic, not a second autonomous controller. No source proof of reliable cognitive diagnosis is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Reconcile the identity/novelty claim of graph propositions extracted from the session against separately read existing graph evidence before committing the update. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate concepts minted by distinct extraction runs or different sessions, same fact paraphrased as different graph nodes, or identity links based on stale graph snapshots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct model auditor explores the existing graph through find/listsessions/listpropositions and submits links; mergeFindings uses those judgments under the audit lock. It cannot inspect or fact-check the raw teaching source. - Decision owner: Separate first-party auditor agent makes semantic identity judgments, with deterministic serialization and merge applying them. No independent raw-source verification or challenge to extractor factual truth is present, so classify a narrow complementary constructor C, not general-purpose closed independent S3 A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Schema-bounded auditor submit, graph exploration tools, process-wide FIFO audit/merge lock, deterministic ledger writes, coverage checkpoints, rollback-on-stage-failure and source provenance. - Closure path: Extractor proposes lesson propositions → isolated auditor sees proposed propositions plus independently queried existing graph → returns identity links → merger collapses duplicate concepts or adds new ones → teacher can subsequently query the updated graph; no verified correction of teaching claims is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Enabled graph extraction invokes runAuditor in the standard source-to-ledger pipeline and mergeFindings consumes its output; BYOK extractor/auditor keys are required. It is a mandatory same-path graph integrity check, not a standing out-of-band lesson review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auditor selects identity links through model exploration rather than code-only deduplication, but its complementary scope is narrow and the raw-source evidence boundary excludes stronger autonomous verification claims. - Claim being audited: Extractor&#39;s implicit assertion that newly proposed graph propositions are unique rather than semantic duplicates of an existing concept. - Ordinary reporting path: Extractor&#39;s submitted proposition list and source-local digest, subsequently represented as new graph nodes. - Complementary access path: Separate graph-aware model agent gets the live existing graph through first-party find/list tools while the extractor is isolated from that graph&#39;s full cross-source state. - Independence boundary: Distinct agent role, prompt and tools; no access to original source text, same application and possible underlying provider model; must not be confused with epistemic truth-checking. - Who acts on findings: The deterministic merger applies links immediately; the resulting graph is read-only exposed to future teacher sessions and the learner. - Evidence: auditor mandate and source exclusion, independent graph exploration tools, graph merge/evidence ledger. - Basis: Active model-backed narrow identity-audit constructor with complementary graph access; no independently validated correctness of the input claims. - Confidence: Medium for S3 constructor, high for its explicit limitations. - Caveats: Headless demo-browser tests occur inside the producing S1 worker and are not independent S3. A routine verifier in the same ingest path should not be promoted to a comprehensive audit of the lesson; future independent same-ref review could reasonably bound this to — if graph identity verification is considered inseparable production validation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would scout changing external/future learning or agent capabilities and return options that alter the lesson organization&#39;s capability rather than just answer a current fact question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool shifts, curriculum/environment changes and novel teaching methods outside the present learner request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Researcher retrieves outside evidence only for teacher&#39;s current informational need; learner/operator selects provider, prompt override and enabled tools by settings. No standing future-oriented strategy process is shown. - Decision owner: Teacher for current accuracy and human for manually changed app settings, not a distinct autonomous S4 opportunity/threat agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search/fetch researcher, durable cross-session knowledge graph, provider roles, prompt/settings UI and saved learner records. - Closure path: External research returns a cited brief into the current lesson and can correct facts, while persistent graph can inform later sessions; neither source path generates and adopts a prospective capability-change strategy. - Boundary reachability: Actual researcher and settings/graph surfaces exist; no future-option-to-S3 contract is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fetching recent facts and cumulative memory do not themselves create an outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: researcher prompt and web tools, teacher research requirement, global model settings. - Basis: Bounded negative: external information serves the current session instead of strategic adaptation. - Confidence: High for reviewed standard path. - Caveats: A user may ask about future trends as a lesson topic; the topic alone does not make its executor S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would resolve an ultimate identity/purpose legitimacy conflict for the learning organization with binding authority and return a governing policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting fundamental educational goals or competing legitimacy claims rather than everyday disagreement about the next lesson step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Learner chooses or changes the study goal and app configuration; teacher&#39;s instructions prescribe a method and ask the learner to approve the plan. No standing binding constitutional-purpose adjudication is wired. - Decision owner: Human learner sets the mission external to the autonomous lesson, teacher regulates subordinate pedagogical commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Probe/goal question, approval before teaching, role prompts, per-user provider and prompt settings. - Closure path: Learner can change the study target, and the next session turn adapts. This is ordinary task definition and plan review, not an adjudicated fundamental identity-policy dispute. - Boundary reachability: Goal elicitation and plan confirmation are active, but identity-level disagreement resolution and binding organizational policy are not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A mandatory teaching philosophy in a system prompt is static implementation policy, not runtime S5 authority. - Evidence: probe/teach split, teacher&#39;s plan confirmation, fixed teaching identity. - Basis: Bounded negative for first-party policy legitimacy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A learner remains the legitimate authority to select its task, but task selection does not on its own justify S5=P at the present recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903/assessments/le/learnharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903\">e0377de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:18:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">exo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo/tree/e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\">e9ad895 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: persistent coding/assistant work with discretionary tool use and delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying tasks, repository state, tool/provider errors and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tools, edits, commands and follow-up steps from current observations. - Decision owner: the main or configured delegated agent in the first-party Loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoreEngine composition, router, tool runner, plugins/approval desk, context tree and typed result persistence. - Closure path: tool results are appended to messages before the next model round; steering and errors update subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: compose.create builds the current core, registers the delegator, connects runtool and starts Loop; no legacy Hermes host is required for this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves tool/transport/persistence mechanisms without task-level transformation decisions. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/loop.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider APIs/claude-cli can supply inference; their unrelated harness functions are not borrowed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 coordination function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic child concurrency and shared-log consistency do not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/attenuation/feedback witness. The writer lock excludes competing cores of the same persistent agent, not mutually adjusted S1 work cells; peer messaging/delegation alone is insufficient. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/core/agentlog.py; src/exo/peers.py; src/exo/core/engine.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organizational control decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task decomposition, narrow task intervention and constructor-selected limits do not independently establish whole-system resource/commitment regulation. The reviewed commands expose current state but no additional function-specific S3 decision/return path beyond ordinary operations was evidenced; no P inferred from a generic operator UI. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/subagents.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/modelspend/policy.py; src/exo/scheduler.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit loop established for current operational claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Trace and findings are first-party observability/self-report with deterministic rechecks; generic critic delegation lacks an audit-specific claim/access/corrective contract. Learning critique and held-out adaptation testing concern prospective proposals, not a separately closed operational audit. No S3 is inferred from audit/reviewer terminology. - Evidence: docs/TRACE.md; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/findings.py; src/exo/core/tools/builtin/harness.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/climb.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future behavior to the external principal&#39;s recurring needs and corrected preferences through typed, evidence-linked options. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current behavior no longer matches principal corrections/rulings or generalizable class-of-task expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: derive a beyond-instance lesson and adopt an eligible future behavior rule; alternatively the principal explicitly accepts/rejects a proposed adaptation. - Decision owner: base mode: aux reviewer/critic supplies adaptive judgment and bounded automatic promotion closes eligible ordinary lessons. Parent mode: legitimate store operator/principal decides the concrete proposal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal authorship filtering, evidence/session/weight constraints, guarded/conflict holds, proposal diffs, promotion ledger and refreshed memory nodes. - Closure path: eligible autonomous promotions or explicit proposalaccept write accepted nodes/skills and ledger state; exo-memory reads nodes fresh for subsequent turns. Guarded/skill paths do not silently bypass their gates. - Boundary reachability: compose attaches learn to the current engine; onturnend starts review for new principal signals; review calls constrained applypolicy. CLI exo proposals exposes show/accept/reject, and accepted nodes return through installed memory. Optional slow API/fixture export is not the basis of automatic A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model develops the prospective behavioral lesson from external principal feedback; deterministic policy constrains its acceptance. In the explicit review mode the principal owns adoption. Mere transcript retention, fact recall or a roadmap is not the credited S4 witness. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/learn/learner.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/promote.py; src/exo/consolidate.py; docs/LEARNING.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: base claim is limited to enabled store-backed, eligible generalizable behavior lessons; facts stored without a prospective behavior implication are not S4. Skill/held-out nightly automatic scheduling is not claimed; no empirical effectiveness or transfer result.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority over the harness&#39;s public/irreversible/financial risk-policy boundaries at the principal recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime gate policy changes could relax reserved authority or alter ultimate allowed exposure/risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adopt or withhold a changed governing gate policy, rather than merely approve one action. - Decision owner: the authenticated live principal; agent/peer input cannot supply that credential. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal transport/authorship checks, pinned policy snapshot, changed-policy hashes, stricter interim enforcement and signed acceptance record. - Closure path: gate status exposes changed policy; /gate accept requires principal authority, records policyaccept and replaces the pinned policy used to govern later calls. - Boundary reachability: installed exo-gate command routes accept to acceptpolicies; the current composed core loads plugins and carries principal authority through the transport path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate policy adoption remains with the principal; deterministic classification, hashes and individual exact-text approval are supporting controls, not autonomous S5. - Evidence: plugins/exo-gate/exogate/hook.py; src/exo/principal.py; src/exo/compose.py; docs/GATE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: bounded to the legitimate principal&#39;s ultimate risk/delegation policy; no claim that every approval, SOUL.md edit or maintainer action is S5. Required-executor behavior is mode-specific. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who may authorize public/irreversible/financial acts and whether changed reserved-risk policy becomes governing. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: principal transport authority checked by requireprincipal; no internal autonomous S5 mode established. - Return-to-operation path: changed policy status/hash → live principal accept → logged policyaccept → new pinned policy → subsequent tool decisions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e/assessments/mo/moissanite-labs-exo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e\">3773544 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-14T00:21:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI-Scientist-v3</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-14\">2026-11-14</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v3/tree/df8313889a004f8a44a472e19a833cd547bb828a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision df8313889a004f8a44a472e19a833cd547bb828a\">df83138 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Conduct an autonomous scientific experiment campaign that chooses and revises candidate research implementations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain hypothesis quality, changing experiment feedback, runtime errors, variable metrics and competing candidate designs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The research agent chooses ideas, modifications and further attempts after evaluating feedback within a task mandate. - Decision owner: First-party invoked scientific research agent; the experiment executor and scoring code are enforcement/evidence mechanisms rather than the owner of idea generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Experiment runner, model tools, persisted campaign state, score/manifest files, worker processes, compute reservation and checkpoint/resume. - Closure path: A selected idea is executed; metrics and failures are returned to the campaign loop; the agent changes the next experiment or selects the candidate to write up. - Boundary reachability: Standard shipped campaign entry path invokes the idea-generation and experiment loop and persists outputs usable by later iterations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the agent retains execution and scoring but not adaptive scientific hypothesis selection or iterative source changes. - Evidence: campaignloop.py, ideageneration.py, mainentry.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: This is autonomy within a researcher-defined task. No live scientific performance or experiment replication was executed for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate operational disturbance between distinct coequal autonomous research S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel workers may race over shared results, GPU resources or duplicate investigative effort; this is a potential concrete interference to check, not proof of an agent-owned coordination function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped S2-specific discretionary coordinator and returned inter-S1 corrective decision was established in the campaign boundary. - Decision owner: Deterministic worker scheduler, cycle lease and host build-lock code; no independently evidenced S2-specific agent owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Thread locks, worker leases, per-round results isolation, shared-memory files and advisory file lock. - Closure path: Scheduling and locking avoid runtime collisions but do not show an autonomous coordination decision that redirects mutually interfering S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker lease assigns execution slots without organizational discretion over relations between separate operations. - Evidence: parallelhelpers.py#L1619-L1754, runtimebuildcoordination.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Govern current whole-system research commitments and resource allocations on behalf of several operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: CPU/GPU scarcity, failed workers and competing experiment candidates create operational control demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supported autonomous or parent-governed whole-system current-control decision distinct from campaign/task execution and fixed resource enforcement is established. - Decision owner: Task configuration/researcher sets experiment budget; deterministic scheduler and pruning routines enforce it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker count, max cycles, GPU pool selection, queue stop flags, score-based pruning and persisted campaign manifests. - Closure path: Runtime budgeting changes how a single campaign proceeds, but an organizational whole-system current-control loop over multiple obligations is not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal experiment selection and fixed budget enforcement are S1 work or infrastructure, not proof of S3 authority. - Whole-system current view: No established S3 actor has a governed view of multiple independent ongoing research commitments beyond single-campaign scheduling. - Current-control decision scope: No established independent S3 intervention right; the inspected scheduler may stop or assign cycles according to preselected rules. - Evidence: parallelhelpers.py, campaignloop.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement routine scientific manuscript reporting with a sufficiently independent inspection of the supporting research evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manuscript may contain unsupported claims or misrepresent data even if routine paper generation and output validation pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct evidence-audit agent judges research claims against assembled evidence, issuing pass/fail findings and requested corrections. - Decision owner: Independent audit-stage agent; parsing, hash checking and loop gates are runtime enforcement rather than the owner of the substantive audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only audit workspace, claim/evidence packet, input SHA-256 verification, validation JSON, failure-code handling and bounded repair rounds. - Closure path: Audit findings are merged into validation feedback; the paper runner revises the manuscript and reruns the audit or records incomplete status after exhaustion. - Boundary reachability: Standard shipped paper.sh calls runagenticevidenceauditgate, builds a read-only workspace and runs a separate auditor stage before accepting manuscript completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the independent audit agent leaves ordinary paper checks but removes the substantive alternative judgment over primary research evidence. - Claim being audited: Manuscript claims accurately and sufficiently reflect the experimental and source evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Researcher&#39;s/author agent&#39;s manuscript, metrics, validation output and final evaluation. - Complementary access path: Separate read-only workspace and evidence-audit packet assembled from paper inputs and source evidence. - Independence boundary: Fresh audit stage cannot simply edit manuscript files, and hashes detect changed inputs after audit preparation; it is not only the manuscript author&#39;s self-reported score. - Who acts on findings: Paper runner converts findings into validation feedback and triggers repair or incomplete status, followed by audit rerun where allowed. - Evidence: paper.sh#L3510-L3610, paper.sh#L4042-L4090, paperevidenceaudit.py#L1819-L1879. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Detect external/future changes relevant to the research organization and adapt the harness&#39;s future operating capability in conversation with current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New scientific papers, research directions and method changes could motivate adaptation beyond the immediate research task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped distinct adaptation-to-harness-capability loop was established; scientific idea generation belongs to the research S1 at this chosen recursion. - Decision owner: Research agent owns task-level hypotheses; researcher/developers outside this runtime choose changes to its organizational mission/capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature retrieval, idea history, agent reflection and experiment results. - Closure path: Research feedback changes individual experiment choices but no separately established external-and-prospective option is returned to whole-system present capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Novel idea search is the declared S1 research service, not automatically S4 adaptation of the service provider&#39;s organization. - External distinction: Available scientific literature and model/tool opportunities are task inputs, but no separate meta-organizational environmental sensing right was confirmed. - Future / prospective distinction: Future scientific opportunities could matter, but the inspected loop optimizes a bounded research campaign. - Adaptation option generated: None shown to update the harness&#39;s organizational capability at the declared recursion. - Path back into current capability / S3: None established beyond task-level experiment iteration. - Evidence: ideageneration.py, idearetryfeedback.py, campaignloop.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Establish and revise ultimate identity-level scientific policy and resolve unresolved S3/S4 tension at the research-system recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A disputed research aim, forbidden methodology or changed institution-level mandate could require ultimate policy judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party organizational constitutional/identity revision path was identified; researcher task/rubric selection is input configuration. - Decision owner: Researcher/provider policy outside this assessed harness; no qualified internal S5 or closed first-party parent authority path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task descriptions, paper-writing instructions, configuration, validation gates and externally set parameters. - Closure path: Agent obeys existing parameters, but an identity-level dispute is not surfaced and closed through legitimate authority to govern later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Merely changing task instructions or criteria is not legitimate ultimate-policy closure under the Profile. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Mission-level dispute, changes to what science the organization is permitted to conduct or the constitutive rules of the research harness. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: None demonstrated as an in-boundary S5 mode; external researcher/developer governance is not borrowed. - Return-to-operation path: No explicit first-party identity-level escalation and authoritative policy return was established. - Evidence: mainentry.py, paper.sh, paperfinalize.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4bec0766c50f05633af8b583db9fdd190a70fa2/assessments/ai/ai-scientist-v3.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4bec0766c50f05633af8b583db9fdd190a70fa2\">a4bec07 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:29:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BINNY</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny/tree/d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\">d0c2ae4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous reverse-engineering investigation and evidence-backed draft construction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown binary behaviour, contradictory strings/function evidence, tool failure, and incomplete classification or report sections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses evidence queries and investigation tool calls, revises hypotheses from results, and authors findings/report sections. - Decision owner: The model actor inside the shipped Engine.Step loop, not deterministic phase transitions or the remote provider&#39;s service operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, static sandbox, STIX/playbook prompts, objective tracking, token caps, context compaction and optional human checkpoints. - Closure path: Model call→first-party tool execution→result in e.messages→subsequent model choice; stages culminate in reviewable draft sections and findings. - Boundary reachability: The TUI&#39;s ordinary investigation command constructs the first-party engine with provider/tool registry and runs Step; no developer-authored coordinator is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime selects budgets and legal tool boundaries, while only model reasoning selects semantically relevant operations against the sample. - Evidence: Engine Step; stage transitions; tool dispatch. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for shipped loop architecture. - Caveats: No live third-party model calls or analyst-domain correctness tests were conducted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct inter-S1 coordination function is shipped at this one-investigation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool sequence and triage/investigate phase changes belong to one S1, not conflict between independent operational cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party ownership/adjustment of competing S1 work is exposed. - Decision owner: None for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stage machine, token caps, project state and report objectives. - Closure path: Tool and phase feedback returns to the same model actor; no inter-unit attenuation return exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct sequential phases or API tools are not distinct S1 units. - Evidence: engine, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Separate analysts can run independent processes, without a shipped cross-run coordination system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-current management of distinct operational commitments/resources is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Per-task token limits, phase checkpoints and model refusals are local investigation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No whole-current bargaining/reallocation or cross-S1 intervention right is present. - Decision owner: Analyst selects autonomy/budgets and the engine enforces caps; model selects local evidence tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxRounds, maxTokens, phase-gates, /autonomy and ContinuePastCap. - Closure path: Cap reached→local pause/analyst extension or stop; no S3 return into an operational whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget enforcement and human override of one analysis are not a distinct S3 function or parent-governed S3 control. - Evidence: engine caps, TUI controls. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high at the selected recursion. - Caveats: Do not infer OpenBin enterprise project management from this CLI&#39;s project selector.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Assertions in a previously produced sample report about functions, addresses, reachability and malware/vulnerability findings. - Ordinary reporting path: The investigation model records findings and report sections during triage/investigate/synthesize, with ordinary tool results in its own context. - Complementary access path: StartQA(report) creates an on-demand skeptical review context, loads report content as claims to check rather than facts and directs fresh raw function/string/xref/reachability reads against source evidence. - Independence boundary: The Q&amp;A mode does not inherit the original evidence as trusted prior work; the model must inspect independently retrieved sample evidence. It is procedural/mode separation, not a separate institution or mandatory different provider. - Who acts on findings: Review-model feedback returns to the analyst; analyst can request recorded correction and /save a revised draft, or use the returned negative evidence for later investigation. - Function: Complementary source-based challenge of ordinary report claims beyond trusting their original narrative. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but false analysis claims, unsupported reachability or section assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In explicitly initiated verification, the model judges the actual sampled evidence as supporting/weakening/refuting a report claim. - Decision owner: Q&amp;A model actor for evidence judgment; analyst owns case selection and final report publication, engine just transports tool results and report edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: qaKickoff skeptical prompt, source read tools, report snapshot and draft editing/saving. - Closure path: Analyst asks to verify→separately started Q&amp;A model reads source→returns a specific finding→analyst may direct corrections/write findings→draft /save returns corrected evidence into the project&#39;s output. - Boundary reachability: /ask and StartQA are shipped user-facing operational modes, not a test fixture or maintainer-only check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model determines whether primary evidence falsifies the report; deterministic tools merely expose bytes/functions. This credits optional agent-owned audit judgment, not autonomous selection of audit targets or remediation. - Evidence: StartQA; skeptical Q&amp;A instruction; README /ask. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for optional complementary review topology. - Caveats: No automatic audit after every draft, provider isolation attestation, or validated remediation was observed; audit depends on an analyst asking concrete questions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No future-oriented external environmental sensing→adaptation options→present capability return is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New malware class or vulnerability questions are investigated as the current deliverable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No standing prospective redesign of BINNY&#39;s own repertoire from environmental trends is exposed. - Decision owner: Developers select playbooks/releases; model picks current-case observations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: STIX classification, playbook selection, provider switching and state persistence. - Closure path: Classifier selects an existing playbook for the current sample; this is S1 task steering, not a forward capability-adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planning and classifying the current case do not constitute externally informed S4 intelligence. - Evidence: playbooks, engine stages. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An analyst may use results to change future practices externally; that arrangement is outside the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure for the agent harness is not supplied as a distinct organizational process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risk, sample access, report publication and model-provider configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No legitimate ultimate mission/policy dispute and returned binding decision path is present. - Decision owner: Analyst/developer selects mode and tool permissions; ordinary report approval is not ultimate authority over identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, API auth, prompts, report draft gate and runtime settings. - Closure path: Permission or save choices affect current case execution/publication; no identity-level governance return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static security/configuration and one draft approval do not establish S5, including the parent mode. - Evidence: README, sandbox, TUI. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is not an assessment of the upstream organization or platform policies.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15/assessments/bi/binny.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15\">f849711 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:48:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PwshHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness/tree/e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\">e56e989 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-owned workspace operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing task requirements, file content, tool failures and shell feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool/arguments and revise subsequent actions or final answer. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed parent agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, tool registry, session lease, exact-action Approve or activated Auto, budget and logging enforcement. - Closure path: Tool result enters the model exchange; the next model request decides continuation; final answer and session records retain outcome. - Boundary reachability: Start-PwshHarness enables shipped agent mode; native tools and continuation are installed by the bundled agent extension. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model the dispatcher and policies retain limits but do not choose task-specific edits and repairs. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for code wiring - Caveats: PowerShell 7.6/provider execution was not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Inter-S1 interference regulation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTasks.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-harness current control is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/Budget.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary challenge of a reported source defect before publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A parent model can mistake a guard/caller/library premise and publish a false or unsupported finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether the concrete claimed defect is supported, refuted, reproduced or unresolved after direct source/counterevidence inspection. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only checker model invoked by reportfinding; runtime parses/records the judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded readonly schemas, fresh provider exchange, exact file/line/trigger/consequence, observed-command provenance and completion policy. - Closure path: Invoke-AgentFindingCheck returns verdict/premise/counterevidence to the parent; Get-AgentFindingReview rejects citing refuted findings and requests checked/qualified reporting in a further step. - Boundary reachability: Bundled AgentTools installs work schemas and completion-review; AgentPresentation dispatches reportfinding to Invoke-AgentFindingCheck; Engine Delegate creates the separate read-only exchange. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model tests the disputed premise using callers/tests/guards, while deterministic parsing and answer reminders only enforce its evidence labels. Generic logs and routine parser/Pester checks are not credited. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentPresentation.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for this bounded audit mode - Caveats: Parent selects the claim and both actors may share provider/model. Independence is fresh reasoning with separate direct source access, not institutional independence; no live witness or universal claim coverage. - Claim being audited: Parent&#39;s reported defect at File/Line with Trigger/Consequence and optional executed reproduction. - Ordinary reporting path: Parent&#39;s own source interpretation and draft final finding. - Complementary access path: Fresh checker reads cited code, callers, guards and tests through independently selected readonly tools and searches for refutation. - Independence boundary: New base messages and separate provider cache/replay, readonly actor, no nested delegates; it receives the claim rather than the parent&#39;s full reasoning transcript. - Who acts on findings: Parent model revises/qualifies its finding; completion-review returns contradictions into subsequent operation when a step/time budget remains.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentLessons.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Authoring.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Approval.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154/assessments/pw/pwshharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154\">17bf101 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:23:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OSWALD</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald/tree/5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\">5a2d610 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute software-change work by interpreting a task, inspecting context, modifying code, generating/testing acceptance evidence and deciding whether the resulting patch is adequate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requests and codebases, code/tool/test failures, ambiguous specifications and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner&#39;s task plan, Coder&#39;s source edits and next tool calls, Tester&#39;s test construction/execution, and Reviewer&#39;s acceptance or blocking critique within the task. - Decision owner: distinct provider-backed role agents invoked in the first-party shipped CLI; model responses choose content and tool actions. The deterministic orchestrator bounds the lifecycle; the human still owns final external publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable worktree base, role-scoped tools, sandbox, artifact schema/persistence, stage timeout and two-attempt cap. - Closure path: Planner spec → Coder model/tool edits → Tester authored/executed tests → Reviewer model verdict → success or findings returned to Coder for a second attempt, or reported stop with saved patch. - Boundary reachability: the default local CLI explicitly constructs all four agent modules and runs them through Orchestrator; it is not an example-only or future-service path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plans, edits, tests and review judgments are model-authored; deterministic stage gates implement those decisions rather than originate task-specific discretion. - Evidence: CLI roles, Planner model/tools, Coder tool loop, Tester commands, orchestrator corrective feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source and decision path; no live LLM/provider run performed. - Caveats: fake test adapters and architecture diagrams do not attest to model quality or autonomous PR publication; operator must separately inspect/apply the saved patch.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop established at the declared one-task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: compatibility between implementation, tests and review comments, rather than independently competing S1 units with an explicit oscillation/conflict regulation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role outputs and correction findings are routed in a fixed sequence; no separately established S2 right mediates a specific inter-unit disturbance. - Decision owner: role agents own stage-level S1 choices and the deterministic pipeline sequences handoffs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed artifact handoffs, stage ordering, test/review gates and bounded retry. - Closure path: a failed test or blocked review returns to the Coder inside the same task loop, not to two or more independently active S1 units through S2 coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: four named agents and a correction edge are not evidence of a separate interference-attenuation function; no first-party inter-S1 mutual adjustment witness is supplied. - Evidence: fixed pipeline and retry, role binding. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported local single-task CLI. - Caveats: separately operated Oswald processes or future web workers are outside the reviewed first-party S2 boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system discretionary current-control loop over a portfolio of operational commitments/resources is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage failure, timeout, unapproved code, worktree and sandbox cleanup, not dynamic whole-system resource allocation or accountable priority conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage progression, retry and stop conditions are fixed in code; model actors can only make task-stage decisions; the operator controls the invocation and external publication. - Decision owner: developer predefines lifecycle/limits; orchestrator enforces them; human retains final integration decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run status/metrics, stagetimeout, two-attempt correction, sandbox isolation, report persistence, cancellation and cleanup. - Closure path: deterministic gate/stop and report/patch return, not a discretionary whole-system current-control decision returning to alter a multi-S1 operational portfolio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the central Orchestrator is named like a manager but its current control is a fixed single-task state machine; progress telemetry does not transfer whole-system regulatory authority to an agent. - Evidence: lifecycle/limits, stop, retry and status, recorded run/metrics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for current CLI boundary. - Caveats: human patch review and planned fleet services are not S3 parent modes in the shipped pipeline.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently examine the Coder&#39;s proposed source changes and Tester evidence for acceptance against a specification, with a blocking corrective verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent implementation defects, self-reported completion or tests that fail to support claimed acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separate Reviewer model chooses APPROVED, CHANGESREQUESTED or rejection with blocking findings against the cumulative source diff, plan and tests. - Decision owner: ReviewerAgent model in the shipped role wiring; the orchestrator validates the verdict and deterministically applies the gate, rather than authoring review judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh manager.getdiff() captured after Tester edits, immutable reviewchanges artifact, read-only Reviewer tool boundary, typed verdict consistency checks and correction cap. - Closure path: cumulative worktree diff plus test evidence → distinct read-only Reviewer model judgment → orchestrator veto or returned findings → Coder correction attempt/terminal fail, with review and patch saved for human inspection. - Boundary reachability: the default CLI always assembles and invokes ReviewerAgent after actual test execution; the diff is freshly read from the worktree, not merely accepted from the Coder&#39;s own completion narrative. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Reviewer produces substantive model-authored blocking issues, while deterministic checks reject internally inconsistent approvals; authoring and audit roles have different inputs/permissions. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s source change satisfies the Planner&#39;s specified work and is supported by tests. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder-produced CodeChanges and Tester-produced TestResults passed into the main pipeline. - Complementary access path: direct cumulative worktree diff via WorktreeManager.getdiff() and separate read-only model inspection of spec, changes and testresults. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a distinct model invocation/role with no write or tool permissions, evaluates actual patch and test artifacts rather than relying solely on Coder status; not an external independent reviewer, separate provider, or independently reexecuted tests. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator returns review blocks to Coder for at most one correction, or refuses success and preserves the terminal report. - Evidence: fresh diff and Reviewer gate, read-only model-authored verdict, reviewer composition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for installed complementary review/feedback topology; no real provider run or measured audit efficacy. - Caveats: audit sees produced test results and patch rather than obtaining separate runtime probes; independence is role/access separation at source level, not an organizationally independent third-party semantic review. No automatic merge authority follows.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external/prospective sensing → adaptation choice → durable current-capability change loop demonstrated in the supported CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unpredictable current software task and its failing tests are handled in S1, not future technology/environment adaptation of Oswald itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner chooses task implementation approach, not a strategic future capability portfolio; no S4 adaptation owner or returned decision is wired. - Decision owner: user selects tasks and maintainers evolve Oswald outside assessed runtime; the agent pipeline cannot promote its patch into Oswald capability autonomously. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task plan, correction findings, saved reports/patch, model-provider configuration and developer roadmap. - Closure path: within-task corrections may improve a candidate patch but do not produce an external-and-future adaptation judgment applied to the harness&#39;s current operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code planning on a supplied request and a human-applied patch are local S1 work, not S4; planned architecture is adjacent. - Evidence: Planner request boundary, no-publish pipeline, future architecture disclaimer. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for supported local mode. - Caveats: third-party users can ask Oswald to implement strategic tooling, but this alone does not give the harness a self-directed S4 decision loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported runtime loop for ultimate purpose/identity-policy conflict and authoritative closure at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary task request constraints, sandbox restrictions and code publication risk, rather than identity/charter tensions between inside-and-now and outside-and-then. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task constraints are supplied by the human and enforced by runtime; no identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to a legitimate parent decision and returned as a governing choice. - Decision owner: user/maintainers choose task, models, sandbox and external patch disposition, but their role is not a first-party closed S5 mode of the task runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration, role prompts, task schema, tool access, test/review gates and human post-run patch review. - Closure path: approval/denial and failure return to the local task, not a policy-identity decision governing the future purpose of Oswald. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed prompts, sandbox/tool restrictions and a human hold before Git mutation are ordinary constraints; neither agent nor parent returns a runtime identity decision. - Evidence: CLI config and role wiring, README human VCS boundary, no commit/merge orchestration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported CLI. - Caveats: upstream OSS maintainer governance is adjacent, and the target end-state human approval API is not credited as an installed S5 parent mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d/assessments/os/oswald.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d\">03af5b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T03:22:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness²</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/tree/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\">1cfe254 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the present task&#39;s harness-editing and probing work through autonomous model-chosen candidate changes, actual executor rollouts and evidence-informed candidate selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen tasks, insufficient baseline harness behavior, different candidate tool/prompt/skill modifications, executable errors and heterogeneous task deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured proposer model chooses what edits to write inside the allowed harness tree; selector model chooses among concrete completed candidate outputs using execution evidence. Those chosen changes are validated and run by the shipped orchestration, with later candidate/edit choices exposed to resulting observations. - Decision owner: Model-backed Proposer/selector operating through first-party runstage, runmode and runselection; external model/runtime implementations supply inference and task execution but do not replace first-party decision/feedback routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python stage scheduler, editable tree validation, candidate/run budget ledger, real adapter execution, retry/fallback, evidence-safe render, filesystem result records and selection persistence. - Closure path: Task/base rollout → proposal-agent harness edit → first-party tree accept/reject → substrate probe with new tree → observed deliverable/trajectory → independent composition/selection decision → chosen current-task output and selection record. - Boundary reachability: Public CLI runsweep → rundomain → runtask → runmode → runstage/real candidate adapter execution → runselection is shipped source. Proposer and selector may be model-backed via released Proposer.call implementations rather than sample-only DemoProposer. No external task executor cognitive function is claimed as a Harness² internal actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party code gives model-backed editor/selector discretion that changes candidate harnesses and selects from observed task outcomes; deterministic scheduler, validator and external benchmark judge are only enforcement/evidence mechanisms. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/proposer.py. - Basis: Reachable configured proposal/edit/probe/compare/select source loop for current task. - Confidence: High for structural mode reachability; no model-backed external benchmark execution attested. - Caveats: Model-less deterministic demo is not itself autonomous; the positive applies only to the supported configured model editor/selector with functional substrate. Absolute benchmark gains are not used as VSM classification evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independent S2 feedback mechanism stabilizes specific interference between separately viable S1 operating units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel task candidates and concurrent domains can compete for execution resources or diverge, but running k proposals/rollouts is controlled experimental scheduling rather than detected inter-S1 organizational conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ThreadPoolExecutor widths, domain ordering, mode-separated namespaces and shared candidate-pool aggregation control concurrency/experiment identity. No agent makes inter-operating-division stabilization decisions that feed back to competing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic task/domain scheduler and user-specified budgets/workers, not a model-owned S2 coordination actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Candidate labels, per-mode Namespace, thread pools, deterministic domain order, cost ledger and output directory partitioning. - Closure path: Proposals/rollouts execute and join → composition/selection. Missing a sensed cross-S1 interference → specific adaptive damping judgment → returned adjustments to each affected autonomous S1 operating division. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task stages and domain jobs are parts of one harness-optimization experiment; concurrent task execution and result aggregation do not automatically establish S2 among independent viable operating organizations. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded source negative after identifying all shipped within-task and across-domain concurrency mechanisms. - Confidence: High for reviewed default modes. - Caveats: An external experiment orchestrator or multi-tenant platform could add S2, but neither is part of the assessed frozen first-party distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-current organization control loop assigns and revises commitments/resources/priorities over multiple independent S1 operating domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task failures, budgets and experimental queue pressure are managed at run scope; no whole-system operational obligations spanning independent current S1 divisions are established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scheduler selects the prespecified task order/mode/budget and handles per-task failures; model selector chooses a task artifact; neither makes a global current-control intervention among distinct operating units. - Decision owner: CLI operator for configuration, deterministic Python scheduler for execution and model editor/selector for task results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Namespace-separated runs, BudgetLedger, result statuses, resume, checked candidate trees and domain task streaming. - Closure path: Configured tasks → bounded execution/selection/summary. Missing whole-organization status → legitimate binding current priority/resource/commitment decision → intervention returned into separate S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Select-best-harness for one task is S1; allocating worker count by human CLI is configuration. Neither is an autonomous or first-party S3 decision over a viable multi-unit current organization. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/ledger.py; harness2/core/selection.py. - Basis: Boundary-relative bounded negative separating experiment management from organization current control. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Research lab/program management may be external; it is not an owned Harness² control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit obtains separate operational reality evidence, challenges S3 assertions and returns authorized corrective S3 action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate harnesses can fail or produce inferior outcomes, and benchmark grader information could leak. First-party tree validation, contrastive probing and independent post-run judge help task-level evidence integrity but do not audit organizational S3 performance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Code validates a tree and denies grader-verdict leakage; a selector judges delivered candidate outputs while an external rubric judge scores outputs in a separate stage. None reports independently sourced whole-current operating contradictions to a legitimate S3 manager with binding correction. - Decision owner: First-party task/evidence validator and choice-model for the current improvement task; external benchmark judge for scoring, not a separate audited management officer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable-tree validator, forbidden-grader evidence checks, candidate pool anonymization, external judgeone, cost ledger and summary artifacts. - Closure path: Candidate output/trajectory → validation and selection/benchmark score → task/result record; missing S3 current report → independent operating observation → complementary challenge → authorized corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contrastive behavioral evidence and result-score separation are real, but output selection for one task is neither S3 evidence independence nor an audit owner with a corrective cross-system path. - Evidence: harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/judge.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicitly considering the grader/selector/evidence-control positive candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high within the frozen app. - Caveats: A dedicated independently observable production system auditor could be built externally; no such shipped owner and returned S3 correction were shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from current encountered environments and adopted task-level harness modifications to autonomously maintain a reusable future-facing improvement library that influences subsequent harness-design decisions across new tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-world tasks arrive that were not known at initial harness construction; improvements for one problem may not transfer directly. Cross-task domain patterns, changing successful component choices and observed errors must inform future harness proposals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After actual current-task rollouts, the model-backed selector picks an adopted harness from candidate evidence and model-backed reflectadoption distills transferable experience. First-party Library.add accepts that model-selected result/lesson into durable per-domain library, and subsequent proposer/editor sessions read it for future harness reconfiguration. - Decision owner: Configured autonomous selector + experience-reflecting model agent inside the shipped runtask/rundomain loop. Deterministic file/library code persists and presents the agent&#39;s chosen strategic material, not an external human deciding each adopted lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain task stream, mode-separated Library, selected harness tree and adoption metadata, contrastive trajectory record, EXPERIENCE.md and INDEX.md, context injection, safe tree validation, leak prevention and retaining only earlier tasks. - Closure path: Environment/task rollouts → model selection of accepted harness → model distills evidence-supported transferable future lesson → library persists full adopted harness + rationale → next task imports library into new model proposer context → its operational harness edits can change under accumulated past experience. - Boundary reachability: A first-party public runsweep invokes rundomain, creates Library objects per mode/domain and executes tasks sequentially. runonemode calls reflectadoption and, with evidence-rich experience, modelib.add; runstage passes librarydir to context.build which actually copies old library context into the next proposer session. No bespoke adopter glue is needed for this supported released mode. - External distinction: Novel tasks in the same domain arrive after prior harnesses have been accepted; actual task-execution contrasts, error patterns and domain-specific cues are fed back through the prior-task experience library rather than assumed from static program configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: The reflector distils a transferable cue/lesson intended for later unknown tasks, and Library stores adopted trees and why they were chosen; this is not the immediate selected deliverable alone. - Adaptation option generated: An autonomous proposer/editor generates new harness-component edits based on stored prior domain experience, and an autonomous selector/reflection loop chooses, explains and retains the successful candidate tree and lesson to inform later alternative edits. - Path back into current capability / S3: Library.add persists the chosen tree and lesson, context.build copies the resulting library into the next proposer work directory, and recursion.runstage hands it to the model edit session whose validated candidate modifies the next task&#39;s executable harness. There is no separate whole-current S3 manager, so the concrete return is direct into next S1 harness-improvement capability at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent makes substantive adoption and transferable-experience decisions from observed external task outcomes; first-party memory storage/return closes them into future edit decisions. This is a prospective reusable improvement-harness capability, distinct from current task&#39;s best-output selection alone. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/recursion.py. - Basis: Installed agent-owned adoption/reflection and persistent cross-task future-harness change feedback, independent of reported benchmark gains. - Confidence: Medium-high for the supported configured cross-task learning mode; effectiveness/generalization not independently tested. - Caveats: This is S4 of the declared harness-improvement organization, not S4 of a separately deployed fixed target coding assistant. Individual future tasks begin from a base harness but the stored adopted earlier trees and model lessons are available to the editing proposer; this is guidance-mediated capability adaptation, not unconditional promotion of a new globally deployed runtime. A failed or empty reflection prevents library admission, so not every task advances S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate legitimate mission/identity-policy arbitration for the harness-improvement organization is shipped as a first-party closed decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-selected benchmark, base model, allowed editable components, executor, task scope and no-leak policies bound optimization but do not entail an identity/purpose governance dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI/research operator fixes the task stream, model/substrate budget and source boundaries; validators enforce prechosen component constraints. No first-party actor chooses legitimate ultimate aims or changes governing ethical purpose and returns those decisions. - Decision owner: External operator/research institution for mission and boundary setup; first-party deterministic config/validation has no identity-level authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmarks, CLI config, read-only rubric protection, editable-component allowlist, ledger and model/executor selection. - Closure path: Configured objective/limits → task improvements in those limits; missing legitimate ultimate policy/identity alternatives → authoritative S5 choice → returned system-wide changed governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical safety and user objective configuration are narrower than governing what the system is and what its ultimate purpose should be. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/spec.py; harness2/core/tree.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; docs/EXPERIMENTS.md. - Basis: Bounded no-first-party identity-purpose governance after inspecting all admissibility and external authority surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Google Research or the deployer may separately exercise organizational S5; not imported as a Harness² runtime ownership claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74/assessments/harness2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74\">9a20d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:03:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Suga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga/tree/88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\">88916d5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce coding/investigation outcomes through the model/tool loop, optionally using read-only research/exploration actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file and command results, tool refusals, provider errors and evolving task observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next tools, delegate exploration, interpret observations and decide the answer or next work. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed principal and its operational child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapters, persistence, workspace/approval guards, transcript/context projection and limits. - Closure path: Model request → chosen tool/agent action → persisted results or child report → next model request/final answer. - Boundary reachability: Current wiring instantiates the first-party kernel and tool set for CLI/ACP; enabled agent tooling builds real read-only child runners rather than borrowing an uninstantiated framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model actors leaves storage, permissions and transport but no task-specific discretionary choices. - Evidence: [Kernel][R1], [current wiring][R2], [child construction][R3], [agent tool][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live inference or agent E2E execution was run. Operational quality and benchmark results are not inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: The local peer-chat protocol attenuates repeated automatic replies between independent workcells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: One session&#39;s peer reply can wake another, whose response wakes the first again without new human work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the anti-reply hop policy and when a held exchange may resume. - Decision owner: The developer/operator supplies maxhops and the continuation policy; the runtime counts/holds. An autonomous coordination-policy actor is not supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sender attribution, TurnHops + 1, configured receiving threshold, held-message queue, message IDs/status and per-turn send cap. - Closure path: Peer reply advances hop depth → receiver reaches maxhops → no new turn is started → held state/status is observable and the message enters only a later human-started turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipped sessionmessage tool is available by default outside disabled/Gofer-job modes, and the real local chat socket delivery path applies the hold rule. This is a first-party anti-oscillation path, not a hypothetical custom mailbox. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model may choose a message, but the decisive coordination threshold/hold rule remains configured; C records the S2-specific construction/control surface rather than autonomous ownership. - Evidence: [Native peer tool][R9], [host message construction][R10], [hop context][R11], [receiver hold][R12], [configuration][R13]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Older peer versions may not support hop gating; the tool reports that limitation. This path does not provide shared-file reservations or coordination for arbitrary external runtimes.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the principal workgroup&#39;s current background operational commitments, including independent subagent tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagents, long commands or watches may remain running, fail, return unhelpful work or consume the current workgroup&#39;s time/resources after priorities change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the current owned-task set, read progress/output, choose which work continues or is killed, and revise subsequent operational action. - Decision owner: Base mode: the principal model-backed agent. Parent mode: the local user supervising that same session/task organization through chat. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-scoped task manager, task snapshots/output/termination, agent run metadata, task notices, cancellation/process-tree handling and configured budget/time ceilings. - Closure path: Task list/output/notice → principal or operator selects current intervention → task kill/cancellation and resulting snapshot/notice → subsequent model work proceeds with revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: CLI/run/chat/ACP wiring installs native task controls when the first-party task manager is present; agent background runs use that manager. Chat&#39;s :tasks/-k commands directly expose the same session task state and termination path to the user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses whether to retain or terminate current owned work; the manager only executes the choice. Configured hard limits do not own those discretionary decisions. Human supervision is separately evidenced, rather than inferred from write approval. - Evidence: [Tool current-view/intervention][R5], [manager List/Kill][R6], [model feedback][R7], [chat view/cancellation][R8], [task wiring][R23], [actor construction][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Scope is one principal session&#39;s owned background workgroup. This does not establish a regulator over all independent chats or external Gofer jobs; a lone foreground run need not exercise this mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party audit-specific independent judgment with corrective current-control return is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect completion claims, missed tests, malformed output or defects in produced artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No installed independent audit owner adjudicates an operational claim through a complementary channel. - Decision owner: The producer and configured delivery checks handle ordinary validation; explore/research children are general operational helpers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Caller-selected --verify command, fixed nudge rules, JSON-schema checks, traces, raw transcript access and read-only child tools. - Closure path: Failed delivery checks re-enter the same producing session. There is no supplied independent audit judgment/authority loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic schema/nudge/verification and generic child exploration are not complementary S3. The config/agents reviewer sketch is not one of the current tool&#39;s instantiated actor types. - Evidence: [Delivery verification][R14], [nudge][R15], [output contract][R16], [actual child types][R3], [agent interface][R4], [current/archive boundary][R21]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A caller can compose an independent reviewer around direct evidence, but that application is not supplied by the reviewed current product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External research results, user feedback and long-session context pressure are operational inputs rather than a supplied future-capability model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No intelligence actor develops adaptation alternatives and closes their return into present capability/current control. - Decision owner: The principal performs requested research and writes permitted memory; user/maintainer selects configuration and releases. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Web research child, feedback/reference memory kinds, summaries, handoff/context segments, idle consolidation and provider settings. - Closure path: Stored memory and compressed context enter later requests; no prospective adaptation-option-to-capability decision path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remembering corrections, summarizing history or doing a current research task does not by itself establish S4. No function-specific parent adaptation loop is evidenced. - Evidence: [Research construction][R3], [memory controls][R19], [idle consolidation][R17], [segment break][R18], [product scope][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Developer optimization/evaluation and archived learning/self-loop documents are not runtime product owners.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Write permissions, external-origin taint, protected paths, agent instructions and memory confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level matter is specifically routed to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a governing policy decision. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies prompt, approval and tool/memory rules; agents own bounded local action choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only registration, strongest approval rules, dangerous-action confirmation, taint guards, rules loading and session state. - Closure path: Allow/deny/confirm outcomes govern actions or memory writes, not ultimate identity or S3–S4 tension resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static rules, user approval and a memory kind called user/feedback are not S5. The narrower user current-task supervision supports S3 only. - Evidence: [Wiring and approval assembly][R2], [child policy snapshot][R3], [memory confirmation][R19], [documented permissions][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The public project/development organization and external Gofer governance are outside this product-level assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55/assessments/su/suga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55\">8068db5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
